/* Chapter 5 — canonical topics, questions, flashcards and exam prompts. */
IFL_DATA.registerChapter({"number":5,"title":"Islamic Law of Contracts and Business Transactions","part":"part-ii","partRoman":"II","pages":[101,128],"minutes":70,"estimates":{"f1":70,"f2":65},"difficulty":"Intermediate","difficultyAlt":"intermediate","learningObjectives":[{"id":"lo-ch5-1","text":"Define Mal, ‘Ain and Dayn, Mabi‘ and Thaman, and the three types of ownership.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch5-2","text":"Distinguish Mithaq, ‘Ahd/Wa‘dah and ‘Aqd, and explain where promises are used by Islamic banks.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch5-3","text":"Identify the elements of a contract and the conditions of offer, acceptance and subject matter.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch5-4","text":"Apply the broad rules for the validity of Mu‘amalat, including the prohibition of two contingent contracts.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch5-5","text":"Explain when a promise is binding (OIC Fiqh Academy conditions), Hamish Jiddiyah and ‘Arbun.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch5-6","text":"Classify contracts as valid (Nafiz/Mawquf, Lazim/Ghair Lazim), Fasid and Batil and state their legal effects.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch5-7","text":"Classify commutative and gratuitous contracts and explain the effect of void conditions on each.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch5-8","text":"Distinguish Mal, usufruct (Manf'at) and ownership, and explain the 'Ain/Dayn and Mithli/Qimi classifications of property.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch5-9","text":"List the elements and components of a valid contract ('Aqd) and the requirements for a proper offer and acceptance.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch5-10","text":"Explain the broad rules governing the validity of Mu'amalat: free consent, avoidance of Gharar, Riba, Qimar/Maisir, and prohibition of two contingent contracts.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch5-11","text":"Describe the status of Wa'dah (promise) in Islamic law, when it becomes binding, and the roles of Hamish Jiddiyah and 'Arbun.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch5-12","text":"Differentiate valid (Sahih), voidable (Fasid) and void (Batil) contracts under the Hanafi classification and their legal consequences.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch5-13","text":"Distinguish commutative (Mu'awadha) from noncommutative (Tabarru') contracts and identify which contracts fall in each category.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch5-14","text":"Explain how conditional/contingent stipulations (T'aliq, Idafa, Iqtiran) affect the validity of a contract.","origin":["f2"]}],"why":"Every Islamic finance product is a bundle of classical contracts. Knowing when a contract is valid, voidable or void — and when a promise binds — is what allows a banker or student to judge whether a Murabaha, Ijarah or Diminishing Musharakah structure is sound.","whyExpanded":"Islamic banks structure every product -- Murabaha, Ijarah, Musharakah, Salam, Istisna'a -- as a chain of underlying contracts, so the general law of contracts covered here is the foundation on which the permissibility of every specific financing mode in later chapters rests. Understanding how Wa'dah becomes binding, how Fasid contracts can be cured, and which conditions are valid or void is essential for structuring Shari'ah-compliant transactions and spotting where a product might collapse into Riba or Gharar.","overview":"Starting from the concepts of wealth (Mal), usufruct and ownership, the chapter sets out the terminology of covenants, promises and contracts, the elements of a contract, broad validity rules, the binding nature of promises (with token money and ‘Arbun), the Hanafi three-way classification of contracts, commutative versus gratuitous contracts, and conditional contracts.","summary":"Islamic finance is in essence an ethical system: what is not prohibited is permissible, and contracts are valid unless they violate the Qur’an, Sunnah or the objectives of Shari’ah. Property is ‘Ain or Dayn; Murabaha applies to ‘Ain and merchandise, not debt documents. Only one counter-value may be postponed — credit sale (Mu’ajjal) or Salam — and debt for debt is prohibited. A valid contract needs form (conforming offer and acceptance), capable parties and a lawful, existing, deliverable, precisely determined subject matter; a sale must be immediate and noncontingent. Two contracts in one are not permissible (hire and purchase cannot be one contract; only a promise to purchase). Commercial promises can be binding, with actual-loss damages. Contracts with immoral or harmful causes are void; Islam blocks all channels leading to injustice.","summaryExpanded":"Chapter 5 lays out the Islamic law of contracts that underlies every Islamic banking product discussed later in the book. It starts from the classification of Mal, usufruct, and ownership, moves through the elements a valid 'Aqd requires (form, parties, subject matter), and the broad validity rules -- free consent, no Gharar, no Riba, no Qimar/Maisir, no prohibited contract-bundling, conformity with Maqasid al-Shari'ah, and profit tied to risk (Al-Ghunm bil-Ghurm). It then examines Wa'dah (promise) and how modern scholarly consensus makes it binding in commercial reliance situations, together with the related devices of Hamish Jiddiyah and 'Arbun. The Hanafi three-way classification of contracts as valid (Sahih), voidable (Fasid), or void (Batil) is explained in detail, along with the practically important distinction between commutative (Mu'awadha) and noncommutative (Tabarru') contracts, and finally the rules on conditional/contingent stipulations.","summarySection":"5.10","takeaways":["Ownership of an asset includes usufruct; usufruct is time-bound.","Three elements: form, parties, subject matter.","Seller must own and possess (physically or constructively) before sale.","Two contingent contracts prohibited; combine via separate contracts and unilateral promises.","Binding promise → actual loss only; HJ before sale, ‘Arbun after.","Sahih / Fasid / Batil — different effects on ownership and price.","Gratuitous contracts carry no charge; service fees amount-based, not time-based.","Mal is classified as 'Ain/Dayn (determinate/indeterminate) and Mithli/Qimi (fungible/nonfungible); ownership can be of the asset, of a debt, or of usufruct only.","A valid contract needs proper offer/acceptance in one Majlis, capable parties, and a lawful, existing, deliverable, precisely specified subject matter with a fixed price.","Mu'amalat validity requires free consent, no Gharar, no Riba, no Qimar/Maisir, no prohibited contract-bundling, conformity with Shari'ah objectives, and profit tied to risk-bearing.","Wa'dah (promise) is not automatically binding, but contemporary consensus enforces it where the promisee relied on it and incurred expense or liability; Hamish Jiddiyah (pre-execution) and 'Arbun (post-execution part-price) are the practical devices banks use.","Hanafi law splits contracts into valid (Sahih -- Nafiz or Mawquf, Lazim or Ghair Lazim), voidable (Fasid -- curable, ownership can pass at market value with consent), and void (Batil -- no legal effect ever, goods always reclaimable).","Commutative (Mu'awadha: sale, hire, Wakalah) contracts allow profit; noncommutative (Tabarru': Qard, Hibah, Kafalah, Hawalah) contracts do not -- banks may only charge separate, amount-based service fees on the latter.","A void condition voids a commutative contract but only falls away itself in a noncompensatory (Tabarru') contract, leaving the underlying contract valid.","Conditional stipulations (T'aliq, Idafa, Iqtiran) are evaluated by whether they support or contradict the contract's purpose and explicit Shari'ah text, not banned outright."],"takeawayOrigins":["f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2"],"checklist":["Can you define ‘Ain, Dayn, Mabi‘, Thaman and the three ownerships?","Can you explain when an offer lapses and the role of Khiyar al-Shart?","Can you list the conditions of the subject matter and define constructive possession?","Can you list the eight broad validity rules?","Can you name permissible contract combinations and their conditions?","Can you state the OIC conditions for binding promises?","Can you distinguish Sahih, Fasid and Batil with their effects?","Can you classify contracts as commutative or gratuitous?"],"topics":[{"id":"t5.1","section":"5.1","title":"Introduction: Why the Law of Contracts Matters","pages":[101,101],"tier":"revision","concepts":["aqd"],"intuition":"Islamic banks trade, lease, partner, guarantee and assign debts. How do we know each deal is valid?","simpleExplanation":"Property is sacred in Islam. Beyond the main prohibitions, each contract type has features that must conform to Shari’ah; non-conforming contracts are invalid. Since IFIs use sale, lease, partnership, suretyship, agency, assignment and mortgage, they need the framework of the Islamic law of contracts.","academicExplanation":["Islam forbids unlawful devouring of others’ property through theft, embezzlement, bribery, cheating and other unlawful means, in addition to Riba, Gharar and Qimar. Contracts not conforming to Shari’ah tenets, or involving prohibited elements, are invalid. The chapter covers general principles, elements, conditions of subject matter, capacity of parties, classification by validity, consideration and causes and effects of invalidity."],"examExplanation":"IFIs deal through contracts (sale, lease, partnership, suretyship, agency, assignment, mortgage); validity per Islamic law of contracts is essential.","keyPoints":["Property is as sacred as life and honour.","Islam treats property as sacred as life and honour — theft, embezzlement, bribery and cheating are forbidden alongside Riba, Gharar and Qimar."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Beyond the big three prohibitions","kind":"practice","text":"A contract free of Riba, Gharar and gambling can still be invalid if it rests on theft, bribery or cheating. Islam protects property as it protects life and honour (p. 101)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t5.2","t5.4"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which of these is NOT among the contracts the chapter lists as used by IFIs?","options":["Sale","Leasing","Gambling","Assignment of debt"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"p. 101."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t5.2","section":"5.2","title":"Mal (Wealth), Usufruct and Ownership","pages":[101,103],"tier":"core","concepts":["mal","ownership","ain-dayn","mithli-qimi"],"intuition":"Before you can sell or lease something, what kind of ‘thing’ is it — and what exactly do you own?","simpleExplanation":"Mal is anything of value that can be possessed and lawfully used (including intangible rights). Property may be ‘Ain (specific) or Dayn (non-specific obligation). Money is Thaman (price), not Mabi‘ (subject of sale). Ownership can be of the asset, of a debt, or of usufruct. Owning an asset includes its usufruct, but not vice versa. Mithli (fungible) goods suit Salam; Qimi (non-fungible) goods suit Istisna‘a.","academicExplanation":["Wealth is anything usable with legal and material value — valuable, possessable and of legitimate use, including abstract rights like trademarks. Fiduciary money is Mal, serving as a medium of exchange and standard of value, but is not itself a subject of sale. Property is movable/immovable, fungible/nonfungible, and determinate (‘Ain) or indeterminate (Dayn). Dayn is the obligation to pay any units of the relevant property equivalent to the obligation (gold, silver, currency, grain).","Islamic law distinguishes Mabi‘ (subject of sale) and Thaman (price). When the same currency is exchanged, notes and debt certificates cannot serve as the thing sold — they count as Thaman (price), like Fulus whose value exceeded intrinsic value.","Ownership categories: Milk ul‘Ain (asset), Milk ud Dayn (debt), Milk ul Manf‘at (usufruct). Ownership of ‘Ain includes its Manf‘at, not the reverse — in Ijarah usufruct passes to the lessee while ownership stays with the lessor; an Ijarah that transfers ownership automatically is void. Milk ul‘Ain is definite and not time-related: a credit seller cannot take back the sold item, only claim the price. Milk ul Manf‘at is time-related, so Ijarah needs a specified period.","Mithli (Zwatul Amthal) goods have similar units (particular varieties of wheat, vehicles of a given trademark); Qimi (Zwatul Qiyam) goods are unique (paintings, gems, buildings). Salam is for Mithli goods; Istisna‘a for Qimi. Bai‘ al ‘Inah (sale and buy-back) is prohibited by the majority; sale and lease-back is allowed by almost all."],"examExplanation":"Mal: valuable, possessable, legitimate use. ‘Ain (specific) vs Dayn (obligation). Mabi‘ vs Thaman (money = Thaman). Ownership: Milk ul‘Ain, Milk ud Dayn, Milk ul Manf‘at; asset ownership includes usufruct, not vice versa; usufruct is time-bound. Mithli → Salam; Qimi → Istisna‘a.","keyPoints":["Intangible rights (trademarks, IP) are Mal.","Money is Thaman, not Mabi‘.","Ijarah with automatic ownership transfer is void.","A credit seller cannot take back sold goods — only claim the price.","‘Inah prohibited by majority; sale and lease-back allowed."],"definitions":[{"term":"Mal","definition":"Anything usable with legal and material value — valuable, possessable and of legitimate use; includes intangible rights."},{"term":"‘Ain / Dayn","definition":"‘Ain: specific, determinate property. Dayn: indeterminate property / an obligation to pay units equivalent to it."},{"term":"Mabi‘ / Thaman","definition":"The subject matter of a sale / the price."},{"term":"Milk ul‘Ain, Milk ud Dayn, Milk ul Manf‘at","definition":"Ownership of an asset, of a debt, and of usufruct respectively."},{"term":"Mithli / Qimi","definition":"Fungible goods with similar units / non-fungible goods whose units differ in value."}],"examples":[{"title":"‘Ain vs Dayn","kind":"textbook","text":"A specific house is ‘Ain (determinate). Gold, silver, currency, grain or oil owed generically are Dayn — any units of the kind may be delivered (pp. 101–102)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Gaining the use of an asset gives ownership of it.","right":"Owning the asset (‘Ain) includes its usufruct, but owning usufruct does not include the asset — as in Ijarah, where the lessor remains owner (p. 102)."}],"relatedTopics":["t5.2.1","t10.4.1","t10.11.2","t11.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which statement about ownership is correct?","options":["Owning usufruct means owning the asset","Owning an asset includes owning its usufruct, but not vice versa","Usufruct ownership is not time-related","Ijarah automatically transfers ownership"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 102."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch5-t1"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch5-t1","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"5.2","sectionTitle":"Mal (Wealth), Usufruct and Ownership","title":"Mal, Usufruct and Ownership","covers":["t5.2","t5.2.1"],"overview":"Contracts deal with Mal (wealth/property), usufruct of goods, and transfer of ownership between parties, each governed by specific juristic classifications.","simpleExplanation":"Mal is anything of value that people are allowed to own and use -- money, goods, buildings. Islamic law splits property into a few pairs of categories: something specific you can point to versus a generic obligation (like a debt), and goods where any unit is interchangeable versus one-of-a-kind items. You can own the thing itself, own a debt owed to you, or just own the right to use something (like a tenant using a rented flat) without owning the thing.","academicExplanation":"Mal is anything usable that has legal and material value, is possessable, and has a legitimate use; it includes intangible rights such as trademarks. Property is classified as movable/immovable, fungible (Mithli)/nonfungible (Qimi), and determinate ('Ain)/indeterminate (Dayn). 'Ain is a specific identifiable item of property; Dayn is a nonspecific obligation to deliver units of a kind of property (e.g. currency, grain) and represents a liability owed by one person to another. Islamic law also distinguishes Mabi' (the object/subject of sale) from Thaman (price/money): currency notes and debt certificates serve as Thaman (a medium of exchange) and cannot themselves be a commodity subject of sale against homogeneous currency, since their exact utility cannot be assessed before being spent. Ownership can take three forms: ownership of the asset itself (Milk ul-'Ain), ownership of a debt (Milk ud-Dayn), and ownership of usufruct (Milk ul-Manf'at). Owning 'Ain automatically carries ownership of its Manf'at (usufruct), but not vice versa -- e.g. in Ijarah the lessee gets usufruct without ownership of the corpus; if a lease contract transfers ownership automatically, it becomes void. Milk ul-'Ain is not time-bound (once owned, ownership persists until validly transferred), whereas Milk ul-Manf'at is inherently time-bound, so a valid Ijarah contract must specify a lease period. The Mithli/Qimi distinction matters operationally: Salam is used for Mithli (fungible) goods, while Istisna'a is used for Qimi (nonfungible, made-to-order) goods.","examExplanation":"Mal = anything of legal/material value, possessable, with legitimate use. Key classifications: 'Ain (determinate) vs Dayn (indeterminate/obligation); Mithli (fungible) vs Qimi (nonfungible); Mabi' (sale object) vs Thaman (price/money -- currency cannot be the Mabi' against homogeneous currency). Ownership types: Milk ul-'Ain (asset), Milk ud-Dayn (debt), Milk ul-Manf'at (usufruct, time-bound). Owning 'Ain gives usufruct too, but usufruct alone does not give ownership of the asset -- this underpins why Ijarah does not transfer ownership.","keyPoints":["Mal must have legal/material value, be possessable, and have a legitimate (Shari'ah-permissible) use.","'Ain = determinate/specific property; Dayn = indeterminate property/obligation owed, settled by any equivalent units.","Mabi' (the sale object) is distinct from Thaman (price); currency notes function as Thaman and are not a valid subject of sale against homogeneous currency.","Three ownership categories: Milk ul-'Ain (asset), Milk ud-Dayn (debt), Milk ul-Manf'at (usufruct).","Getting ownership of the asset ('Ain) brings ownership of its usufruct, but getting usufruct alone does not transfer ownership of the asset -- this is why Ijarah keeps ownership with the lessor.","Milk ul-'Ain is not time-bound; Milk ul-Manf'at is inherently time-bound, so Ijarah must specify a lease period.","Mithli (fungible) vs Qimi (nonfungible) goods: Salam is used for Mithli goods, Istisna'a for Qimi goods.","Bai' al-'Inah (sale and buy-back) is prohibited by the majority; sale-and-lease-back is allowed by almost all jurists."],"definitions":[{"term":"Mal","definition":"Anything that can be possessed; includes money (gold, silver, monetary units), commodities, and immovable properties -- it must be of value, possessable, and have a legitimate use."},{"term":"'Ain","definition":"Determinate or specific property (as opposed to Dayn)."},{"term":"Dayn","definition":"Indeterminate/nonspecific property, or a liability/obligation of one person to another to be fulfilled with equivalent units of a kind of property."},{"term":"Mithli","definition":"Fungible goods -- all units are similar (e.g. wheat of a given variety) and interchangeable."},{"term":"Qimi","definition":"Nonfungible goods -- each unit has a different value (e.g. paintings, gems, buildings)."},{"term":"Milk ul-Manf'at","definition":"Ownership of usufruct -- the right to use an asset for a specified time, as against ownership of the asset itself."}],"conditions":[],"principles":["Ownership of 'Ain (the asset) automatically carries ownership of its Manf'at (usufruct), but ownership of usufruct alone does not transfer ownership of the underlying asset.","Milk ul-Manf'at is inherently time-bound; a valid Ijarah must stipulate a lease period."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Ijarah and non-transfer of ownership","kind":"textbook","text":"In an Ijarah contract, usufruct passes to the lessee but ownership of the asset stays with the lessor; if the lease is structured so ownership transfers automatically as a consequence of the lease itself, the contract becomes void."}],"commonConfusions":["Students often assume 'owning the use of something' is the same as owning the thing -- the book stresses these are legally distinct (Milk ul-'Ain vs Milk ul-Manf'at).","Currency is sometimes wrongly treated as an ordinary tradeable commodity; the text explains it functions as Thaman (price/medium of exchange), not as Mabi' against a homogeneous currency."],"distinctions":["'Ain (determinate) vs Dayn (indeterminate/obligation)","Mabi' (sale object) vs Thaman (price)","Mithli (fungible, used for Salam) vs Qimi (nonfungible, used for Istisna'a)"],"relatedConcepts":["Ijarah","Salam","Istisna'a","Bai' al-'Inah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.2","pages":[101,102,103,104]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Milk ul‘Ain","b":"Milk ul Manf‘at","text":"Asset ownership is definite and not time-bound and includes usufruct; usufruct ownership is time-bound and does not include the asset."},{"a":"Mithli goods","b":"Qimi goods","text":"Mithli (e.g. a wheat variety) can be the subject of Salam; Qimi (e.g. a building) is used in Istisna‘a."}]},{"id":"t5.2.1","section":"5.2.1","title":"Defining Related Terms: Mithaq, ‘Ahd/Wa‘dah and ‘Aqd","pages":[103,105],"tier":"core","concepts":["aqd","wad"],"intuition":"A covenant, a promise and a contract all involve commitment. How are they different — and which do Islamic banks use?","simpleExplanation":"Mithaq is a solemn covenant (treaties, marriage). ‘Ahd or Wa‘dah is a (usually unilateral) promise. ‘Aqd is a contract: offer and acceptance joined so that they affect the subject matter. Islamic banks use promises in MPO, Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek, sale and lease-back, Diminishing Musharakah and disposal of Salam/Istisna‘a goods, and contracts on both deposit and asset sides.","academicExplanation":["Mithaq: an earnest covenant with more sanctity than ordinary contracts — covenant with God (13:20), treaties (8:72; 4:90), marriage (4:21); more relevant to religious and social covenants. ‘Ahd: a unilateral promise or undertaking, sometimes bilateral; termed Wa‘dah in Fiqh literature.","‘Aqd (lexically conjunction/tie) = contract. Definitions: conjunction of an offer from one party with acceptance by the other affecting the subject matter (Murshid al-Hayran); undertaking obligations by two parties via Ijab and Qabul (Majallah); a legal relationship from two declarations (Al ‘Inayah); concurrence of two wills to create, shift or relinquish an obligation (Sanhuri). General sense: any earnest act, even unilateral (Waqf, debt remission); specific sense: offer plus acceptance with legal consequences.","Promises used by IFIs: MPO (client promises to buy at cost plus profit); Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek and Ijarah Sukuk (promise to sell/buy/gift at the end); sale and lease-back; Diminishing Musharakah (promise to purchase units); disposal of Salam and Istisna‘a goods. Contracts: Amanah, Qard, Shirkah, Wakalah with depositors; Bai‘, Ijarah, Ujrah, Shirkah, Wakalah, Kafalah, Ju‘alah and Hawalah with fund users."],"examExplanation":"Mithaq = solemn covenant; ‘Ahd/Wa‘dah = promise (unilateral); ‘Aqd = contract (Ijab + Qabul with legal effects). Promises in MPO, IMBT, sale-lease-back, DM, Salam/Istisna‘a disposal.","keyPoints":["‘Aqd has general and specific senses.","Promise is a key tool alongside contracts.","Promises (Wa‘dah) are used in MPO, Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek, sale and lease-back, Diminishing Musharakah and the disposal of Salam/Istisna goods."],"definitions":[{"term":"Mithaq","definition":"Covenant — earnest, firm determination to fulfil obligations, more sacred than ordinary contracts."},{"term":"‘Ahd / Wa‘dah","definition":"A unilateral promise or undertaking (sometimes bilateral)."},{"term":"‘Aqd","definition":"Contract: conjunction of offer (Ijab) and acceptance (Qabul) creating legal consequences for the subject matter."}],"examples":[{"title":"Two senses of ‘Aqd","kind":"textbook","text":"In the general sense ‘Aqd covers any earnest undertaking, even unilateral (Waqf, remission of debt); in the specific sense it is offer plus acceptance producing legal effects (p. 104)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t5.6","t9.8.2","t11.5.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which term best matches ‘a unilateral promise or undertaking’?","options":["Mithaq","‘Ahd/Wa‘dah","‘Aqd","Qabul"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 103."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t5.3","section":"5.3","title":"General Framework of Contracts","pages":[105,106],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["general-permissibility","aqd"],"intuition":"Why are jurists relatively free to design new contracts, but not new forms of worship?","simpleExplanation":"Ibadat (worship) are fixed by express command; Mu‘amalat (transactions) are permissible unless prohibited. In transactions, the apparent wording of the contract governs, not hidden intent. Contracts with an immoral or harmful underlying purpose are void.","academicExplanation":["Ibadat are universal truths unaffected by time; Mu‘amalat may change with time and space. Ibn Taymiyah: devotional acts need express sanction, while transactions are governed by permissibility and absence of prohibition — giving jurists liberty to solve emerging issues. Businesses operate as sole proprietorship, Shirkah, Wakalah, Ujrah, or through sale and lease, subject to rules constituting the Islamic law of contracts. In Mu‘amalat the apparent wording or writing has legal effect; a party cannot plead a different intent (Niyyah) — whereas in Ibadat intent matters. The motivating cause must accord with Shari’ah: contracts promoting immorality, against public policy or harmful to third parties are void (e.g. selling a weapon to a known criminal)."],"examExplanation":"Ibadat: express sanction needed. Mu‘amalat: permissible unless prohibited (Ibn Taymiyah). In transactions, apparent wording governs, not Niyyah. Immoral/harmful purpose → void.","keyPoints":["Apparent wording governs in Mu‘amalat.","Selling a weapon to a known criminal is invalid.","In Mu‘amalat, the apparent wording has legal effect — a party cannot later plead a different intention; in Ibadat the intention (Niyyah) matters."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Selling a weapon to a known criminal","kind":"textbook","text":"Sale or hire of a weapon to someone the seller knows will kill innocents is invalid: the motivating cause of a contract must accord with Shari’ah (p. 106)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t2.2.1","t5.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In Mu‘amalat, which has legal consequence?","options":["The hidden intention (Niyyah) only","The apparent wording/format of the contract","Neither","Only verbal statements"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 105."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch5-t2"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch5-t2","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"5.3","sectionTitle":"General Framework of Contracts","title":"Ibadat vs Mu'amalat and the General Framework of Contracts","covers":["t5.3"],"overview":"Islamic law distinguishes devotional acts (Ibadat), which require express sanction, from transactions (Mu'amalat), which are governed by a presumption of permissibility.","simpleExplanation":"In worship, you can only do what's specifically commanded. In business dealings, it's the opposite: everything is allowed unless it's specifically forbidden. What matters in a business contract is what was actually said and agreed (the words), not what someone secretly intended -- the opposite of worship, where inner intention (Niyyah) is what counts.","academicExplanation":"Ibn Taymiyah's distinction, cited by Ayub, is that Ibadat (devotional acts) are sanctioned by express injunctions -- what is not commanded cannot be made obligatory -- while for Mu'amalat (transactions) the governing principle is permissibility and absence of prohibition: nothing is prohibited unless proscribed by Allah and His Prophet. This gives jurists reasonable latitude to address emerging financial problems. A further rule distinguishes Mu'amalat from Ibadat on evidentiary grounds: in Mu'amalat the apparent wording/format of the contract has legal effect -- a party cannot later claim the contract did not reflect his true intent (Niyyah); the law enforces what was actually agreed. In Ibadat, by contrast, intent (Niyyah) is what matters, not mere words. Contract validity also requires that its underlying motivating cause conform to the Shari'ah: contracts promoting immorality, harming a third party, or against public policy are void (e.g. selling or hiring a weapon to someone known to intend murder).","examExplanation":"Ibadat = express-sanction principle (only what is commanded is required); Mu'amalat = permissibility principle (everything allowed unless expressly prohibited) -- this is the root of 'Ibahatul Asliyah'. In Mu'amalat, the apparent wording/form of the contract governs (not secret intent); in Ibadat, intent (Niyyah) governs. A contract whose underlying purpose is unlawful (e.g. facilitating a crime) is void even if its form is correct.","keyPoints":["Ibadat (devotional acts) require express Shari'ah sanction; Mu'amalat (transactions) are governed by the presumption of permissibility unless expressly prohibited.","This distinction (from Ibn Taymiyah) gives jurists latitude to solve new financial problems within the Mu'amalat framework.","In Mu'amalat, the apparent wording/form of the contract has legal effect; a party cannot disclaim it by citing unexpressed intent (Niyyah).","In Ibadat, intent (Niyyah) itself is what is judged, not mere outward words.","A contract's underlying motivating cause must conform to the Shari'ah; contracts that promote immorality, harm third parties, or are against public policy are void.","Mu'amalat covers both social and economic/financial activities; the chapter focuses on the economic/financial category."],"definitions":[{"term":"Ibadat","definition":"Devotional acts -- universal truths unaffected by time and space, sanctioned by express injunctions of the Shari'ah."},{"term":"Mu'amalat","definition":"Transactions between individuals in worldly matters, which may change with time and place, and are governed by the principle of permissibility unless expressly prohibited."},{"term":"Niyyah","definition":"Intent or intention while doing an act -- decisive in Ibadat, but not what governs the enforceability of a Mu'amalat contract's stated terms."}],"conditions":["A contract's underlying motivating cause must be Shari'ah-compliant; it must not promote immorality, be against public policy, or harm a third party's person or property."],"principles":["Ibahatul Asliyah: general permissibility -- all economic activities not prohibited by the Qur'an and Sunnah are valid.","In Mu'amalat, apparent contract wording (not secret intent) is legally binding."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Sale of a weapon to a known criminal","kind":"textbook","text":"A sale or hiring of a weapon to a criminal who the seller/lessor knows intends to use it to kill innocent people is invalid, because the underlying purpose of the transaction is unlawful."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes assume Islamic contract law is restrictive like Ibadat (only what's explicitly allowed is permitted); the text is explicit that Mu'amalat works the opposite way."],"distinctions":["Ibadat (express-sanction principle) vs Mu'amalat (permissibility principle)"],"relatedConcepts":["Ibahatul Asliyah","Shari'ah objectives (Maqasid)"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.3","pages":[105,106]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Ibadat","b":"Mu‘amalat","text":"Ibadat require express sanction and intent matters; Mu‘amalat are permissible unless prohibited and the apparent wording governs."}]},{"id":"t5.4","section":"5.4","title":"Elements of a Contract","pages":[106,106],"tier":"core","concepts":["aqd"],"intuition":"What must be present for any contract to exist?","simpleExplanation":"Three essential elements: the form (offer and acceptance — Sighah), the contracting parties (‘Aqidain), and the subject matter (Ma‘qud ‘alayh). Sanhuri lists seven components.","academicExplanation":["A contract requires two capable (mature, sane) parties, offer and acceptance, a legal basis of union between the declarations and obligations, and freedom from prohibited factors. Jurists generally hold three essential elements — Sighah (form), ‘Aqidain (parties), Ma‘qud ‘alayh (subject matter); absent these the contract is invalid. Sanhuri’s seven: concurrence of offer and acceptance; unity of Majlis; plurality of parties; sanity/power of distinction; subject matter susceptible to delivery; defined object (Mahall); beneficial nature of the object (trade permitted)."],"examExplanation":"Three elements: Sighah (offer & acceptance), ‘Aqidain (parties), Ma‘qud ‘alayh (subject matter). Sanhuri’s seven components.","keyPoints":["Parties must be mature and sane.","Sanhuri lists seven components: concurrence of offer and acceptance, unity of Majlis, plurality of parties, sanity, deliverable subject matter, defined object, and permissible (beneficial) object."],"definitions":[{"term":"Sighah","definition":"Form of the contract — offer and acceptance."},{"term":"‘Aqidain","definition":"The contracting parties."},{"term":"Ma‘qud ‘alayh","definition":"The subject matter of the contract."}],"examples":[{"title":"Missing element","kind":"practice","text":"A seller signs a sale contract for “some goods from my warehouse” at a price to be decided later. The form (offer and acceptance) exists, but the subject matter and price are undefined, so an essential element is defective (p. 106)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t5.4.1","t5.4.2","t6.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which is NOT one of the three essential elements of a contract?","options":["Sighah","‘Aqidain","Ma‘qud ‘alayh","Witnesses"],"correctAnswer":3,"explanation":"p. 106."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch5-t3"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch5-t3","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"5.4","sectionTitle":"Elements of a Contract","title":"Elements of a Contract: Form, Parties, Subject Matter","covers":["t5.4","t5.4.1","t5.4.2"],"overview":"A valid 'Aqd requires proper offer and acceptance (Sighah), qualified contracting parties ('Aqidain), and a valid subject matter (Ma'qud 'alayh), each with detailed conditions.","simpleExplanation":"For a contract to count, three things must be in place: someone has to make a clear offer and the other side has to clearly accept it (in the same sitting), both people need to be mentally capable adults, and what's being bought/sold or agreed on has to be a real thing you can identify, that's allowed to trade, and that the seller can actually hand over.","academicExplanation":"The three essential elements of a contract are the form (Sighah, i.e. offer/Ijab and acceptance/Qabul), the contracting parties ('Aqidain), and the subject matter (Ma'qud 'alayh). Sanhuri's seven-component list adds unity of Majlis (contracting session), plurality of parties, sanity/capacity, deliverability of subject matter, a defined object (Mahall), and its beneficial/lawful nature. Offer (Ijab) is a firm proposal to create an obligation; acceptance (Qabul) must conform to the offer in all its details and, per the Hadith 'the contracting parties have the right of option until they separate,' must occur within the same Majlis (session) -- a requirement some scholars interpret liberally so that time reasonably taken to communicate acceptance still counts as the same 'meeting.' Offer and acceptance can be conveyed by words, gesture, or conduct; an offer lapses on withdrawal, death/incapacity of a party, termination of the Majlis without conclusion, destruction of the subject matter, or lapse of a stipulated acceptance period. The unity-of-session rule does not apply to agency, gift, or appointment of an executor for a minor's property. Regarding subject matter, it must be existing/existable, valuable, usable, capable of ownership and delivery, specified/quantified, and the seller must hold title and bear its risk; a nonexistent thing cannot be validly sold (hence short-selling is prohibited) except under the narrow, conditions-based exceptions of Salam and Istisna'a. The subject matter must also be intrinsically lawful (not wine, pork, intoxicants), free of legal charge (e.g. a mortgaged asset cannot be sold until redeemed), fit for the contract's purpose (public roads/parks cannot be sold), and not harmful. Precise determination can be by direct inspection or by sufficiently detailed description; if goods are present, majority view requires examination. The price/Thaman must be fixed and certain at contract time -- an uncertain or floating price (e.g. 'I will tell you the price later' or a price that varies by payment date without one final figure being fixed) voids the contract. Finally, possession (Qabza), physical or constructive (Hukmi), is required before on-selling -- 'he who buys foodstuff should not sell it until he has taken possession of it.'","examExplanation":"Three elements: Sighah (offer+acceptance), 'Aqidain (capable parties), Ma'qud 'alayh (lawful, existing, deliverable, specified subject matter with a fixed price). Offer/acceptance must match and occur in one Majlis; lapses on withdrawal, death, session ending, destruction of subject, or time lapse. Subject matter must exist, be owned, lawful, deliverable and precisely determined; price must be certain -- Gharar in price or subject voids the sale. Possession (actual or constructive/Hukmi) is required before resale (basis for the Salam/short-sale exceptions and rules).","keyPoints":["Three essential elements: form (offer & acceptance), contracting parties, subject matter -- all must satisfy their conditions or the contract is invalid.","Offer (Ijab) and acceptance (Qabul) must conform to each other and normally occur within the same Majlis (contracting session).","An offer lapses on withdrawal, death/incapacity, end of session without agreement, destruction of subject matter, or expiry of a stipulated acceptance period.","Unity-of-session does not apply to agency, gift, or appointment of a minor's executor.","Subject matter must exist (or be exempted like Salam), be valuable, usable, ownable, deliverable, lawful, free of legal charge, and precisely specified.","A nonexistent or undeliverable item (e.g. a stolen car, a missing animal) cannot be sold -- basis for the prohibition of short-selling.","The price must be certain and fixed at the time of contract; an open-ended or later-determined price voids the sale.","Possession (physical or constructive/Hukmi) of the subject matter by the seller is required; goods bought must be possessed before being resold."],"definitions":[{"term":"Sighah","definition":"The form of a contract -- the offer (Ijab) and its acceptance (Qabul)."},{"term":"'Aqidain","definition":"The two contracting parties to a contract."},{"term":"Ma'qud 'alayh","definition":"The subject matter of a contract."},{"term":"Majlis","definition":"The contracting session/meeting; offer and acceptance normally must occur within the same Majlis for the contract to be concluded."},{"term":"Qabza (Hukmi)","definition":"Constructive possession -- a situation where the buyer has not taken physical delivery, but the commodity's risk and control (and all rights/liabilities) have already passed to him."}],"conditions":["Offer must be in clear, unconditional language.","Acceptance must conform to the offer in all its details and (generally) occur in the same Majlis.","Contracting parties must be mature and sane (capable of contracting).","Subject matter must be existing/existable, valuable, lawful, ownable, deliverable, specified/quantified, and free of legal charge.","The price (Thaman) must be certain and fixed at the time of the contract.","The seller must have title to, and possession (actual or constructive) of, the subject matter before selling it onward."],"principles":[],"processSteps":["Offer (Ijab) made by one party in clear terms.","Acceptance (Qabul) given by the other party, conforming to the offer, within the same Majlis.","Confirmation that both parties are sane, mature, and capable of contracting.","Verification that the subject matter is lawful, existing/existable, specified, and deliverable, and the price is fixed.","Transfer of ownership (with risk and reward) occurs at the concluding event (e.g. handshake or signature)."],"examples":[{"title":"Uncertain price voids the sale","kind":"textbook","text":"If a seller says 'take this and I will charge you the market price' or 'I shall tell you the price later,' and the buyer agrees without one final price being stipulated, the transaction is void for Gharar in price."},{"title":"Sale of an unidentified shop","kind":"textbook","text":"An owner of a shopping mall who says 'I sell one of the shops to you' without identifying which shop makes the contract voidable, unless the specific shop is pointed out or identified."}],"commonConfusions":["Students confuse 'unity of session' with requiring instant, on-the-spot acceptance; the Federal Shariat Court's view cited in the text clarifies that time genuinely needed to think or inquire (e.g. before buying a factory) can still be part of the same Majlis.","Sale of a debt/receivable is void for Gharar (the debtor's repayment is uncertain) unless structured through Hawalah with recourse to the original debtor -- students often miss this narrow carve-out."],"distinctions":["Physical possession vs constructive (Hukmi) possession","Sale of a nonexistent thing (generally void) vs Salam/Istisna'a (narrow Shari'ah-sanctioned exceptions with conditions removing Gharar)"],"relatedConcepts":["Gharar","Salam","Istisna'a","Hawalah","Khiyar al-Shart"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.4","pages":[106,107,108,109,110]}}]},{"id":"t5.4.1","section":"5.4.1","title":"Offer and Acceptance: Form of the Contract","pages":[106,108],"tier":"core","concepts":["aqd","khiyar"],"intuition":"When exactly is a deal concluded — and what if the buyer needs time to think?","simpleExplanation":"The offer (Ijab) is the first firm proposal; acceptance (Qabul) must match it and come in the same session (Majlis). Words, gestures or conduct can convey them. Offers lapse on withdrawal, death/incapacity, end of the session, destruction of the subject or lapse of time. Khiyar al-Shart lets a party take time.","academicExplanation":["The offer must be clear and unconditional; offer and acceptance must conform on subject matter and consideration and be issued in the same session. Ijab is the firm proposal made first; Qabul the subsequent declaration. Whether abstention can be Ijab is debated: Pakistan’s Council of Islamic Ideology says only commission of an act; the Federal Shariat Court accepts abstention (like the Contract Act 1872). Offer and acceptance may be by words, gesture/indication or conduct; no particular words are fixed.","An offer is cancelled by withdrawal; death or loss of capacity; termination of the Majlis without conclusion; destruction of the subject matter; lapse of the time fixed for acceptance. Unity of session derives from the Hadith “the contracting parties have the option until they separate”. Khiyar al-Shart (option of stipulation) overcomes the unity-of-session restriction by making the contract nonbinding for a specified period. The FSC held a narrow reading of Majlis impractical for large transactions (e.g. buying a factory); to some modern scholars, the ‘meeting’ is a legal fiction covering the time to communicate acceptance. If the buyer obtains a specified time from the seller, the sale may be concluded within it. Unity of session does not apply to agency, gift and appointment of an executor."],"examExplanation":"Ijab (first firm proposal) + Qabul (conforming acceptance) in the same Majlis; by words, gesture or conduct. Offer lapses: withdrawal, death/incapacity, end of Majlis, destruction, time lapse. Khiyar al-Shart gives time. Unity of session not needed for agency, gift, executor.","keyPoints":["Acceptance must conform to the offer in all details.","Five causes of cancellation of an offer.","FSC: flexible interpretation of Majlis for large transactions."],"definitions":[{"term":"Ijab / Qabul","definition":"Offer (firm proposal made first) / acceptance (subsequent declaration conforming to the offer)."},{"term":"Majlis","definition":"The session/meeting of the contract; offer and acceptance should occur in it."},{"term":"Khiyar al-Shart","definition":"Option of stipulation — makes a contract nonbinding for the holder during a specified period."}],"examples":[{"title":"Selling a factory","kind":"textbook","text":"The Federal Shariat Court observed that a strict one-session Majlis would make large deals like buying a factory impossible, since title, machinery, liabilities and profitability must be examined; time given for acceptance keeps the offer alive (p. 107)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Unity of session applies to every contract.","right":"It does not apply to agency, gift and appointing an executor for a minor’s property (p. 108)."}],"relatedTopics":["t6.12","t5.4"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which of these does NOT cancel an offer?","options":["Withdrawal by the offeror","End of the session without acceptance","Destruction of the subject matter","The offeree asking a clarifying question within the session"],"correctAnswer":3,"explanation":"p. 107."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"processSteps":["Offer (Ijab) made clearly and unconditionally","Acceptance (Qabul) conforming in all details","Within the same session (or within time granted/Khiyar)","Contract concluded — formal event (e.g. signature) marks transfer"]},{"id":"t5.4.2","section":"5.4.2","title":"Elements of the Subject Matter","pages":[108,110],"tier":"core","concepts":["qabd","gharar","bai-dayn"],"intuition":"What must be true of the thing being sold? Existence, value, ownership, possession, deliverability and precise specification.","simpleExplanation":"The subject matter must exist (or be existable, as in Salam), be valuable, lawful, owned, free of charges, deliverable and precisely specified by seeing or description; the price must be fixed. The seller must possess it (physically or constructively) before selling. Short-selling and sale of debt (except via Hawalah) are invalid.","academicExplanation":["The subject matter should be existing/existable, valuable, usable, capable of ownership, capable of delivery/possession, specified and quantified, and the seller must have title and risk. Selling a nonexistent thing is void even with consent; short-selling is prohibited by almost all scholars. Usufruct is property and can be sold. A missing animal or stolen car cannot be sold. Sale of debt is invalid (seller does not know if/when the debtor pays) unless subject to Hawalah with recourse; Salam is allowed because details are pre-agreed.","Conditions: pure, lawful materials of value (no wine, pork, intoxicants), purpose not contrary to Maqasid (no brothel or gambling house); owned and free from legal charge (a mortgaged asset cannot be sold until redeemed); fulfils the contract’s objective (perishables cannot be pledged; public roads cannot be sold); not harmful.","Precise determination by pointing/examination (if present in the session, the majority require examination) or by description (sufficient if the asset is unique; otherwise the unit must be identified — “one of the shops” is voidable). Price must be agreed and fixed; “market price” or “100 in one month, 105 in two” without choosing is invalid; the unit of price must be known. A formal event (handshake, signature) marks conclusion and transfer of ownership, risk and reward.","Capacity to deliver is essential. “He who buys foodstuff should not sell it until he has taken possession” — the vendor must be the real owner bearing risk and reward. Possession (Qabza) may be physical or constructive (Hukmi): the commodity is in the possessor’s risk and control with rights and liabilities passing to him, including risk of destruction; for immovables, legal notice or mutation suffices. Salam requires commodities normally available at delivery; in Istisna‘a the manufacturer must supply."],"examExplanation":"Subject matter: existent, valuable, lawful, owned, unencumbered, deliverable, specified (by sight or description), price fixed. Possession (Qabza) physical or constructive before resale. No short-selling; debt sale only via Hawalah with recourse.","keyPoints":["Constructive possession = risk and control transferred.","Mortgaged asset cannot be sold until redemption.","“One of the shops” is voidable unless identified.","Formal event (signature) marks transfer of risk and reward."],"definitions":[{"term":"Qabza (possession)","definition":"Physical or constructive possession of the subject matter by the seller before sale."},{"term":"Constructive (Hukmi) possession","definition":"The commodity has come into the possessor’s risk and control and all its rights and liabilities, including risk of destruction, have passed to him without physical delivery."}],"examples":[{"title":"“I sell you one of the shops”","kind":"textbook","text":"A mall owner saying “I sell one of the shops to you” makes a voidable contract unless the shop is specifically identified (p. 109)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A bank can sell goods it has ordered but not yet received or taken risk of.","right":"The seller must own and possess (physically or constructively) the goods — i.e. bear their risk — before selling."}],"relatedTopics":["t6.5.1","t9.6","t7.16"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which describes constructive possession?","options":["Holding the invoice only","The commodity is in the possessor’s risk and control with rights and liabilities passed to him","Having paid a deposit","A promise to buy"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 110."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"conditions":["Exists (or existable as in Salam)","Lawful and of value","Owned by the seller and free from legal charge","In the seller’s physical or constructive possession","Deliverable","Precisely determined (seen or described)","Price agreed and fixed in a known unit"]},{"id":"t5.5","section":"5.5","title":"Broad Rules for the Validity of Mu‘amalat","pages":[110,114],"tier":"core","concepts":["gharar","riba","maisir","maqasid","general-permissibility"],"intuition":"Is there a checklist to test any transaction? The author gives eight broad rules.","simpleExplanation":"Valid transactions need: free mutual consent; no Gharar; no Riba; no gambling; no two mutually contingent contracts; conformity with Maqasid; profit only with liability; and the general rule that anything not prohibited is permitted.","academicExplanation":["Free mutual consent (5.5.1): consent obtained through oppression, fraud or misperception invalidates; parties need definite knowledge of the subject matter and obligations; inspection and documentation (especially of credit) are encouraged. Najash, Ghaban-e-Fahish, Talaqqi-al-Rukban and concealing defects or value-related information in trust sales like Murabaha are prohibited.","Prohibition of Gharar (5.5.2): required in commutative contracts; some uncertainty is affordable in gifts. The commodity must exist, be owned and possessed; Salam and Istisna‘a are the only exceptions, with conditions removing Gharar. Sale of debt even at face value is not allowed — hence bill discounting is not allowed — but Hawalah with recourse validates it. Short-selling, conventional derivatives, insurance, difference-settled futures in shares and FX, and trading in shares of provisionally listed companies involve Gharar. Speculation per se (buying/selling with future price changes in mind) is not prohibited; only sales of nonexistent/not-owned goods and Maisir are.","Avoiding Riba (5.5.3): IFIs must earn by pricing goods or usufruct, not by lending; otherwise income goes to charity. Avoiding Qimar and Maisir (5.5.4): no lotteries or pure-luck prize schemes. Conformity with Maqasid (5.5.6): contracts must not militate against the objectives or public benefit. Permissibility (5.5.8): all agreements and conditions are permissible unless they contradict an explicit text; credit (Mu’ajjal) and forward (Salam) transactions have their own rules."],"examExplanation":"Eight rules: free consent; no Gharar; no Riba; no Qimar/Maisir; no two contingent contracts; conformity with Maqasid; profit with liability; permissibility as general rule. Speculation per se not prohibited; debt sale only via Hawalah.","keyPoints":["Talaqqi-al-Rukban: buying from Bedouins before they reach the market — prohibited.","Salam and Istisna‘a are the only exceptions to existence/ownership/possession.","Bill discounting not allowed; Hawalah with recourse is.","Speculation per se is not prohibited.","Speculation per se (buying with an eye to future price changes) is not prohibited; only sales of non-existent/unowned goods and gambling are.","Discounting bills is not allowed; subjecting a debt to Hawalah (recourse to the original debtor) validates the transfer."],"definitions":[{"term":"Talaqqi-al-Rukban","definition":"A city dweller buying a Bedouin’s goods at a far lower price before the latter reaches the market — prohibited."}],"examples":[{"title":"Talaqqi al-Rukban","kind":"textbook","text":"A trader rides out to meet Bedouin sellers and buys their goods before they reach the market and learn the prices. This is prohibited, because consent obtained through their ignorance of market value is not truly free (pp. 110–114)."},{"title":"Bill discounting vs Hawalah","kind":"textbook","text":"Selling a 100,000 receivable for 95,000 in cash is sale of debt at a discount, which is not allowed. Assigning it at face value, with recourse to the original debtor (Hawalah), is valid (pp. 110–114)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"All speculation is prohibited in Islamic finance.","right":"Speculation per se — trading in view of possible price changes — is not prohibited; only sales involving nonexistent/not-owned goods or Maisir are."}],"relatedTopics":["t5.5.5","t5.5.7","t3.2","t2.2.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"According to Section 5.5.2, how can a sale of debt be validated?","options":["By discounting it","By subjecting it to the rules of Hawalah with recourse to the original debtor","By adding a premium","It can never be validated"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 111."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch5-t4"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch5-t4","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"5.5","sectionTitle":"Broad Rules for the Validity of Mu'amalat: Consent, Gharar, Riba, Qimar/Maisir","title":"Free Consent, Gharar, Riba and Qimar/Maisir as Validity Requirements","covers":["t5.5","t5.5.5","t5.5.7"],"overview":"Valid Mu'amalat require free mutual consent and must be free of excessive uncertainty (Gharar), interest (Riba), and games of chance (Qimar/Maisir).","simpleExplanation":"For a deal to count in Islamic law, both sides must genuinely agree (not tricked or pressured), both sides must know clearly what they're getting and what they're paying, there must be no interest charged on money, and there must be no gambling-style luck deciding who wins or loses.","academicExplanation":"Free mutual consent requires that parties knowingly and certainly understand the subject matter and their rights/obligations; consent obtained through oppression, fraud or misperception invalidates a contract. Practices like Najash (false bidding to inflate prices), Ghaban-e-Fahish (charging an exorbitant price while implying it is the market price), Talaqqi-al-Rukban (a city dweller exploiting a Bedouin's ignorance of market prices before he reaches the market), and concealment of material defects (especially in trust sales like Murabaha) are strictly prohibited. Gharar (excessive uncertainty about subject matter or price) particularly affects commutative contracts (gratuitous contracts like gifts can tolerate some uncertainty); examples include the sale of fish still in water or unripe/undefined future factory output. To avoid Gharar, the commodity must exist, be owned, and be in the seller's possession at the time of sale -- Salam and Istisna'a are the only Shari'ah-sanctioned exceptions, validated by conditions (precise quality, quantity, price, delivery time/place) that remove the uncertainty. Sale of debt is likewise void for Gharar (the debt purchaser bears the risk of the debtor defaulting) -- so discounting of bills is impermissible -- unless structured via Hawalah (assignment of debt) with recourse to the original debtor. Other Gharar-based prohibited transactions cited: short-selling of shares, conventional derivatives, insurance, and futures/speculative trading where only a price difference is netted without real delivery. Genuine speculation (buying/selling anticipating price change) is not itself prohibited -- only sale of nonexistent/unowned goods and Maisir/Qimar are. Riba is an increase with no corresponding consideration in an exchange or a loan; Islamic banks must avoid it in both trading and lending activities, earning income instead through pricing of goods/usufruct, not lending. Qimar (games of chance -- gain purely from luck) and Maisir (getting profit too easily, without work) are prohibited outright -- covering lotteries, prize schemes based purely on luck, dicing and wagering.","examExplanation":"Validity requires: (1) free mutual consent -- no Najash, Ghaban-e-Fahish, Talaqqi-al-Rukban, or concealment; (2) no Gharar -- subject matter/price must be certain, item must exist/be owned/be possessed (exceptions: Salam, Istisna'a, via specific curing conditions); sale of debt is Gharar-void unless via Hawalah with recourse; (3) no Riba -- no unearned increase in exchange or loan; (4) no Qimar/Maisir -- no gain purely from chance/luck. Genuine speculation about future prices is not itself prohibited; only Gharar-laden or Maisir-based deals are.","keyPoints":["Free mutual consent requires certain, definite knowledge of subject matter and obligations; consent from oppression, fraud, or misperception voids a contract.","Najash, Ghaban-e-Fahish, and Talaqqi-al-Rukban are prohibited practices that undermine free consent.","Gharar chiefly threatens commutative contracts; gratuitous contracts (e.g. gifts) tolerate some uncertainty.","Salam and Istisna'a are the sole Shari'ah exceptions to 'the item must exist and be possessed,' validated by conditions removing Gharar.","Sale of debt is void for Gharar unless structured as Hawalah with recourse to the original debtor; bill discounting is not allowed.","Short-selling, conventional derivatives, and insurance (as discussed) are cited as Gharar-based prohibited transactions; mere price speculation is not itself prohibited.","Riba is any increase without corresponding consideration in an exchange or loan; Islamic banks earn via trade/lease pricing, not lending.","Qimar and Maisir (gambling, lotteries, wagering, prize schemes based on luck) are prohibited outright."],"definitions":[{"term":"Gharar","definition":"Excessive uncertainty relating to major elements of a contract -- the subject matter, its consequence, or its price -- leading to potential dispute or unjust enrichment."},{"term":"Najash","definition":"Bidding up the price without an intention to purchase and take delivery of the commodity."},{"term":"Ghaban-e-Fahish","definition":"Excessive profiteering with deception -- charging an exorbitant price while implying the market price is being charged."},{"term":"Qimar","definition":"Games of chance -- gambling -- where possession of property is contingent on an uncertain event."},{"term":"Maisir","definition":"Getting something too easily, or a profit without working for it."}],"conditions":["Consent must be free of oppression, fraud, and misperception.","Parties must have certain, definite knowledge of the subject matter and their rights/obligations.","Subject matter and price must be free of excessive uncertainty (Gharar).","No increase without corresponding consideration (Riba) may be built into an exchange or loan.","No element of pure-chance gain or loss (Qimar/Maisir) may be present."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Discounting of bills is not allowed","kind":"textbook","text":"Because the sale of a debt is void for Gharar (uncertainty about whether/when the debtor will pay), discounting of bills is not permitted unless structured via Hawalah with recourse to the original debtor."}],"commonConfusions":["Speculation itself (anticipating future price movement) is not prohibited; what's prohibited is speculation that involves selling nonexistent/unowned goods or that amounts to Maisir/Qimar.","Students sometimes think Gharar bans all uncertainty; the text is explicit that gratuitous (noncompensatory) contracts can tolerate some uncertainty, and that only excessive/major uncertainty in commutative contracts is prohibited."],"distinctions":["Gharar (uncertainty) vs Riba (unearned increase) vs Qimar/Maisir (chance-based gain) -- three distinct prohibitions that often co-occur","Sale of debt (generally void) vs Hawalah with recourse (valid workaround)"],"relatedConcepts":["Salam","Istisna'a","Hawalah","Bai' al-Dayn"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5","pages":[110,111,112]}}],"subsections":[{"number":"5.5.1","title":"Free Mutual Consent","page":110,"points":["No coercion, fraud or misperception","Knowledge of subject and obligations","No Najash, Ghaban-e-Fahish, Talaqqi-al-Rukban, concealment"]},{"number":"5.5.2","title":"Prohibition of Gharar","page":110,"points":["Existence, ownership, possession required","Salam/Istisna‘a exceptions","No debt sale except Hawalah; speculation per se allowed"]},{"number":"5.5.3","title":"Avoiding Riba","page":111,"points":["Profit from pricing goods/usufruct, not lending"]},{"number":"5.5.4","title":"Avoiding Qimar and Maisir","page":112,"points":["No luck-based schemes"]},{"number":"5.5.6","title":"Conformity with the Maqasid","page":113,"points":["No contract against public benefit"]},{"number":"5.5.8","title":"Permissibility as a General Rule","page":114,"points":["Conditions valid unless contradicting explicit text"]}]},{"id":"t5.5.5","section":"5.5.5","title":"Prohibition of Two Mutually Contingent Contracts","pages":[112,113],"tier":"core","concepts":["two-contracts","ijarah","diminishing-musharakah"],"intuition":"Islamic products often combine contracts. When is a combination allowed and when is it ‘two deals in one’?","simpleExplanation":"Prohibited: forcing purchase of a second article; one article at two prices without choosing; contingent sale; combining sale and loan. Keep separate contracts. Allowed combinations: sale and lease kept separate with a unilateral promise; Shirkah with Ijarah; Musharakah with Mudarabah; Wakalah/Kafalah with sale or lease; Salam/Istisna‘a with Murabaha. Debt-for-debt exchanges are prohibited, hence full prepayment in Salam.","academicExplanation":["The Prophet (pbuh) prohibited: (1) selling two articles so that buying one obliges buying the other; (2) one article for two prices without stipulating one; (3) contingent sale; (4) combining sale and lending. Jurists prefer each contract to relate to one transaction; separate contracts rather than one mixed contract.","Permissible combinations: Bai‘ and Ijarah must be separate — in Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek the bank remains lessor and bears ownership risks and expenses throughout; transferring ownership risk to the lessee voids the transaction; a unilateral promise to sell, buy or gift at the end is allowed (not binding on the other party). Shirkah and Ijarah: a partner may lease his undivided share to a co-partner (unanimous); in Diminishing Musharakah each unit sale needs separate offer and acceptance. Musharakah and Mudarabah: a bank managing depositors’ funds (Mudarabah) may add its own funds, but a sleeping partner’s profit ratio cannot exceed its capital ratio. Wakalah and Kafalah with sale/lease, per their rules (Wakalah is part of Murabaha, Salam and Istisna‘a). Salam or Istisna‘a with Murabaha for preshipment export finance; Diminishing Musharakah combines Shirkah and Ijarah with an undertaking.","Exchange of two liabilities is prohibited. Ibn Rushd identifies nine kinds of sale (corporeal/corporeal, corporeal/liability, liability/liability; each immediate or delayed). Delay on both sides is debt for debt — prohibited — hence full prepayment in Salam."],"examExplanation":"Prohibited: forced second purchase; two prices unchosen; contingent sale; sale + loan. Allowed combos with separate contracts: sale & lease (unilateral promise), Shirkah & Ijarah, Musharakah & Mudarabah, Wakalah/Kafalah with sale/lease, Salam/Istisna‘a + Murabaha. Debt-for-debt prohibited → Salam price fully prepaid.","keyPoints":["IMBT: transferring ownership risk to lessee during the lease voids it.","Leasing one’s undivided share to a co-partner is unanimously allowed.","Sleeping partner’s profit ratio ≤ its capital ratio.","Debt-for-debt exchange prohibited.","In Diminishing Musharakah, each sale of units needs its own offer and acceptance."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Ibn Rushd’s nine kinds of exchange","kind":"textbook","text":"Exchanges of property for property, property for liability, or liability for liability can each be immediate for both, delayed for both or immediate for one. Delay on both sides is a debt-for-debt exchange and is prohibited — hence full prepayment in Salam (p. 113)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Combining any two contracts is always prohibited.","right":"Sale with separate lease, Shirkah with Ijarah, Musharakah with Mudarabah, and Wakalah/Kafalah with sale are permissible if each contract keeps its own rights and liabilities (pp. 112–113)."}],"relatedTopics":["t6.8","t11.4.5","t12.9.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why must the price in Salam be paid in full in advance?","options":["Tradition only","Delay on both sides would be exchange of debt for debt, which is prohibited","To earn interest","It is optional"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 113."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch5-t5"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch5-t5","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"5.5","sectionTitle":"Prohibition of Two Contingent Contracts, Maqasid, Profit-with-Liability, Permissibility","title":"Contingent Contract Combinations, Maqasid, Profit-with-Liability and General Permissibility","covers":["t5.5","t5.5.5","t5.5.7"],"overview":"Beyond consent/Gharar/Riba/Qimar, valid Mu'amalat must avoid combining two mutually contingent contracts, must align with the Maqasid (objectives) of the Shari'ah, must tie profit to risk-bearing, and otherwise benefit from a general presumption of permissibility.","simpleExplanation":"You can't bundle two deals together so that agreeing to one forces you into the other, or leave a sale with two possible prices without picking one. Contracts must serve the bigger goals of Islamic law (like protecting people's property and wellbeing), and you can only earn profit if you're also taking on real risk -- otherwise it's just like interest. Finally, remember the default rule: anything not specifically banned is allowed.","academicExplanation":"The Prophet prohibited combining two mutually contingent/inconsistent contracts, covering: (1) forcing purchase of a second article to get a first; (2) selling one article for two unstipulated prices; (3) contingent sale; (4) combining sale and lending in one contract. Jurists therefore prefer that a sale relate to only one transaction. However, certain combinations of contract types are permissible under conditions: Bai' and Ijarah must be kept as separate agreements (so Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek keeps the lessor/lessee relationship throughout the lease, with the bank bearing ownership risk, and only a unilateral, nonbinding-on-the-other-party promise to transfer at lease end); Shirkah and Ijarah can combine (a partner may lease his share to a co-partner, though sale of ownership units in Diminishing Musharakah requires separate offer/acceptance per unit); Musharakah and Mudarabah can combine (e.g. a bank deploying its own funds alongside Mudarabah deposits, provided a sleeping partner's profit ratio doesn't exceed its capital ratio); Wakalah and Kafalah can be combined with sale/lease contracts, each governed by its own rules. Exchange of two liabilities (debt for debt, with delay on both sides) is prohibited -- hence full prepayment is required for a valid Salam. Conformity with Maqasid al-Shari'ah requires that no contract undermine the broader welfare objectives of the Shari'ah (the 'rights of Allah' correspond to public rights in modern law) -- a contract against the public interest is invalid. Profit-with-liability (a corollary of Al-Ghunm bil-Ghurm) means a person is entitled to profit only if he bears business risk; any excess paid to a creditor above principal is prohibited because the creditor bears no business risk. Finally, Permissibility as a General Rule (Ibahatul Asliyah) holds that everything not prohibited is permissible, provided agreements/conditions don't contradict explicit Qur'an/Sunnah text -- this underlies the validity of deferred-payment (Mu'ajjal) and forward (Salam) transactions, subject to their specific validating rules.","examExplanation":"Prohibited: two contingent contracts bundled (forced second purchase, unstipulated dual price, contingent sale, sale+loan combo); exchange of two delayed liabilities (debt for debt) -- hence Salam needs full prepayment. Permissible combinations (with conditions): Bai'+Ijarah kept separate (basis of Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek); Shirkah+Ijarah; Musharakah+Mudarabah; Wakalah/Kafalah with sale/lease. Maqasid conformity: no contract may work against Shari'ah's broader welfare objectives/public rights. Profit-with-liability (Al-Ghunm bil-Ghurm): profit requires risk-bearing -- root justification for prohibiting interest. Ibahatul Asliyah: everything not expressly prohibited is permissible.","keyPoints":["Prohibited combination types: forced dual purchase, unstipulated dual pricing, contingent sale, sale-plus-lending in one contract.","Debt-for-debt exchange (delay on both sides) is prohibited -- this is why Salam requires full advance payment.","Bai' and Ijarah must remain separate contracts; only a unilateral, non-binding-on-the-other-party promise may link them (basis of Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek).","Shirkah+Ijarah, Musharakah+Mudarabah, and Wakalah/Kafalah with sale/lease are permissible combinations under specific conditions.","Maqasid al-Shari'ah: any contract against the broader welfare objectives/public interest of the Shari'ah is invalid.","Profit-with-liability (Al-Ghunm bil-Ghurm): profit is legitimate only where the earner bears business risk -- basis for prohibiting a guaranteed return on a loan.","Ibahatul Asliyah (Permissibility as a general rule): everything not expressly prohibited by the Qur'an/Sunnah is permissible, underlying credit (Mu'ajjal) and forward (Salam) transactions."],"definitions":[{"term":"Al-Ghunm bil-Ghurm","definition":"Earning profit is legitimized only by risk-sharing and engaging in an economic venture; one can claim profit only if ready to bear business liability/risk."},{"term":"Ibahatul Asliyah","definition":"General permissibility -- all economic activities not prohibited by the Qur'an and Sunnah are valid/permissible."}],"conditions":["A sale contract should relate to only one transaction; separate deals should use separate contracts.","Where Bai' and Ijarah are combined in a bank product, they must remain legally separate, with only a unilateral, nonbinding promise linking them.","A sleeping partner's (e.g. depositor's) profit ratio in a combined Musharakah/Mudarabah cannot exceed the ratio its capital bears to total capital.","No contract may be against the broader public welfare objectives (Maqasid) of the Shari'ah.","Profit can only be claimed by a party bearing corresponding business risk/liability."],"principles":["Al-Ghunm bil-Ghurm / Kharaj bi-al-Daman: profit accompanies liability for loss.","Ibahatul Asliyah: permissibility is the default rule for Mu'amalat."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek structure","kind":"textbook","text":"Throughout the lease period the relationship stays lessor-lessee (the bank bears ownership risk/expenses); a separate unilateral promise (not a binding condition of the lease) allows transfer of ownership at the end via a distinct sale or gift contract."}],"commonConfusions":["Students often think combining any two contract types is banned outright; the text clarifies several combinations (Shirkah+Ijarah, Musharakah+Mudarabah, Wakalah/Kafalah+sale/lease) are allowed under conditions -- what's banned is contingency/bundling that creates Gharar or effectively merges sale with lending."],"distinctions":["A binding condition linking two contracts (prohibited) vs a unilateral, nonbinding promise linking them (permitted, as in Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek)"],"relatedConcepts":["Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek","Diminishing Musharakah","Salam"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5","pages":[112,113,114]}}],"table":{"caption":"Permissible combinations (Section 5.5.5)","head":["Combination","Condition"],"rows":[["Sale + Ijarah","Separate agreements; bank bears ownership risk during lease; unilateral promise at end"],["Shirkah + Ijarah","Partner may lease undivided share; unit sales in DM need separate offer and acceptance"],["Musharakah + Mudarabah","Sleeping partner’s profit ratio cannot exceed capital ratio"],["Wakalah/Kafalah + sale/lease","Rights and liabilities per each contract’s rules"],["Salam/Istisna‘a + Murabaha","Each contract’s conditions fulfilled (e.g. preshipment export finance)"]]}},{"id":"t5.5.7","section":"5.5.7","title":"Profits with Liability","pages":[113,113],"tier":"core","concepts":["kharaj-daman"],"intuition":"Why does a creditor get no profit but a seller or lessor does?","simpleExplanation":"A person is entitled to profit only when bearing the risk of loss — in sale, hire and partnership. A creditor bears no business risk on the amount lent, so any excess is prohibited.","academicExplanation":["This principle operates in sale, hire and partnership contracts. Any excess paid to the creditor is prohibited because the creditor does not bear business risk on the amount lent. In sale and lease, parties bear risk as each contract requires.","A person is entitled to profit only when he bears the risk of loss. The principle operates in sale, hire and partnership. The creditor bears no business risk on the amount lent, so any excess paid by the debtor over the principal is prohibited. In sale and lease, each party bears the risk required by the respective contract — the seller until delivery, the lessor throughout the lease (p. 113)."],"examExplanation":"Profit only with liability for loss; creditor bears no business risk → no excess.","keyPoints":["Applies to sale, hire and partnership."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Why a lender cannot share profit","kind":"textbook","text":"A lender gets back his full principal whatever the borrower does with it, so he has no risk and no claim to profit; a partner who wants a share of profit must stand to lose his capital (p. 113)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t4.2.6"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why is a creditor not entitled to profit on a loan?","options":["Loans are small","The creditor bears no business risk on the amount lent","It is customary","Only for consumption loans"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 113."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"distinctions":[{"a":"Creditor","b":"Partner / seller / lessor","text":"The creditor’s principal is guaranteed, so no return; the partner, seller and lessor bear the risks of their contracts and are entitled to profit, price or rent."}]},{"id":"t5.6","section":"5.6","title":"Wa‘dah (Promise) and Related Matters","pages":[114,116],"tier":"core","concepts":["wad"],"intuition":"Islamic banks rely on a customer’s promise to buy. Is that promise enforceable — and what can the bank recover?","simpleExplanation":"Contemporary scholars agree promises in commerce are enforceable unless force majeure prevents fulfilment. If the promisor wilfully backs out, he must make good the actual loss (not opportunity cost). The OIC Fiqh Academy sets four conditions. Bilateral promises are generally not allowed where the contract itself would not be allowed (e.g. forward currency).","academicExplanation":["‘Ahd generally does not create legal obligation but becomes binding where the promisee incurs expenses or liability because of it; contingent promises are also binding. Contemporary scholars have reached consensus that Wa‘dah is enforceable unless force majeure prevents it; if broken wilfully, the promisor makes good the loss. Example: A promises to sell a house to bank B for $100,000 but sells to C — A covers B’s actual loss. A asks B to buy a car and promises to buy it for $20,000; B buys for $18,000, A backs out, B sells for $17,000 — A bears the $1,000 net loss, recoverable from security or token money.","Rationale: binding promises are a genuine need that does not violate basic tenets — e.g. FX cover via unilateral promise with simultaneous exchange later. Dylan Ray argued medieval Fiqh supports non-binding promises and that modern practice creates conflict; the author disagrees: Malikis, Hanbalis, some Hanafis and Shafi‘is and almost all contemporary jurists accept binding promises in commercial dealings with mutual consent (views of Samurah b. Jundub, Umar b. Abdul Aziz, Hasan al-Basri, Imam Bukhari, Ibn Shubruma, al-Ghazzali; a Maliki view binds only where the promisee incurred expenses). It contradicts no Nass and is acceptable under Ibahatul Asliyah.","OIC Fiqh Academy conditions: (1) unilateral; (2) the promisor caused the promisee to incur liabilities/expenses; (3) if a promise to purchase, the actual sale takes place at the appointed time by offer and acceptance — the promise is not the sale; (4) on backing out, the court may force purchase or actual damages — actual monetary loss, excluding opportunity cost. Muwa‘adah (bilateral promise) is not allowed by the majority where ‘Aqd is not allowed (e.g. forward currency); some subcontinent scholars enforce bilateral promises except for short-selling of currencies or shares. Unlike ‘Aqd, a promise does not transfer ownership. Implications: MPO, Ijarah-wal-Iqtina‘, Diminishing Musharakah, disposal of Salam/Istisna‘a goods."],"examExplanation":"Promise binding (contemporary consensus) unless force majeure; wilful breach → actual loss (not opportunity cost). OIC conditions: unilateral; promisee incurred liability; actual sale by separate offer/acceptance; court may enforce purchase or damages. Bilateral promise not allowed where contract not allowed.","keyPoints":["Promise ≠ sale: ownership does not transfer.","Damages exclude opportunity cost/cost of funds.","Muwa‘adah not allowed where ‘Aqd is not allowed (majority).","Author rejects Dylan Ray’s ‘conflict’ critique."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Car purchase promise","kind":"textbook","text":"A promises to buy a car from bank B for $20,000. B buys it for $18,000; A backs out; B sells it for $17,000. A must bear the $1,000 actual loss, recoverable from security or Hamish Jiddiyah. B cannot claim its expected $2,000 profit."},{"title":"Promise to buy a car","kind":"textbook","text":"A asks bank B to buy a car and promises to buy it at $20,000. B buys it for $18,000; A backs out and B sells it for $17,000. A bears the $1,000 actual loss, recoverable from his security or token money (p. 114)."},{"title":"Promise to sell a house","kind":"textbook","text":"A promises to sell a house to a bank next month for $100,000 but sells it to C. A is liable for the bank’s actual costs incurred in reliance on the promise (p. 114)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t3.3.2","t9.8.2","t5.6.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Under the OIC Fiqh Academy conditions, what may a bank recover when a client breaks a promise to purchase?","options":["Expected profit","Actual monetary loss, excluding opportunity cost","Interest on funds","Nothing"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 115."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch5-t6"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch5-t6","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"5.6","sectionTitle":"Wa'dah (Promise) and Related Matters","title":"Wa'dah (Promise): Binding Status, Hamish Jiddiyah and 'Arbun","covers":["t5.6","t5.6.1"],"overview":"Wa'dah ('Ahd) generally does not create legal obligation, but contemporary scholarly consensus treats it as enforceable in commercial dealings when the promisee has incurred expense or liability relying on it; Hamish Jiddiyah and 'Arbun are related security devices.","simpleExplanation":"A promise to do something in the future isn't automatically a binding contract in Islamic law. But if you make someone rely on your promise (they spend money or take on obligations because you promised), most scholars today say you must keep it -- or pay for the actual loss you caused. Banks often take a deposit ('earnest money') from a customer who promises to buy something, so that if the customer backs out, the bank can cover its real losses from that deposit.","academicExplanation":"Wa'dah/'Ahd generally does not create a legally enforceable obligation, but becomes binding where the promisee has incurred expenses or taken on liability as a result of relying on the promise; contingent promises are also considered binding. Contemporary scholarly consensus (reflected in an OIC Islamic Fiqh Academy resolution) makes a unilateral commercial promise binding where: (1) it is unilateral; (2) the promisor has caused the promisee to incur liabilities/expenses; (3) if it is a promise to purchase, the actual sale must still occur via a fresh offer and acceptance at the appointed time (the promise itself is not the sale); (4) if the promisor reneges, courts may compel performance or award actual damages (excluding opportunity cost). Ayub notes Ray's critique that medieval Fiqh sources historically held promises nonbinding, and Ayub's own rebuttal (citing Usmani) that many traditional jurists (Malikis, Hanbalis, some Hanafi/Shafi'e) and virtually all contemporary jurists already accepted binding promise in genuine commercial dealings with mutual consent -- so there is no real conflict with classical Fiqh. Muwa'adah/Mu'ahidah (bilateral promise) is, per the majority, not enforceable where the underlying 'Aqd itself would be impermissible (e.g. forward currency contracts), though some subcontinent scholars treat bilateral promise as enforceable except in cases like short-selling of currencies/shares. A key legal distinction: in a bilateral promise, ownership does not transfer at the time of the promise (unlike in an actual 'Aqd, where ownership transfers immediately along with inheritance rules applying). Practically, banks take Hamish Jiddiyah (token/earnest money reflecting the promisee's seriousness) before execution of the agreement, held as a trust and adjusted into the price at execution; if the promisee defaults, the bank may recover its actual loss (excluding cost-of-funds) from this amount, refunding any excess. 'Arbun differs in timing: it is taken from the buyer as part of the price after execution of the sale agreement, and (per the majority of the Hanbali school, who alone treat Bai' al-'Arbun itself as a valid independent contract) the seller may retain the whole 'Arbun if the buyer fails to complete the purchase within the stipulated period (though AAOIFI prefers refunding any amount beyond the seller's actual loss).","examExplanation":"Wa'dah: generally nonbinding, but binding when the promisee relied on it (incurred expense/liability) -- contemporary OIC Fiqh Academy conditions: unilateral, promisee incurred liability, actual sale still needs fresh offer/acceptance, court can compel purchase or award actual damages (no opportunity cost) on default. Muwa'adah (bilateral promise) generally unenforceable where the underlying 'Aqd itself would be impermissible. Hamish Jiddiyah = earnest money taken BEFORE execution of agreement (trust, adjusted into price, actual-loss recovery on default). 'Arbun = part-payment taken AFTER execution of sale agreement (forfeitable if buyer backs out, per Hanbali view; AAOIFI prefers capping forfeiture at actual loss).","keyPoints":["Wa'dah/'Ahd is generally not legally binding by default, but becomes enforceable when the promisee has relied on it by incurring expense or liability.","OIC Fiqh Academy conditions for a binding commercial promise: unilateral; promisee incurred liability/expense; actual sale still requires fresh offer & acceptance; default remedy is compelled performance or actual damages (excluding opportunity cost).","Bilateral promise (Muwa'adah) is generally unenforceable where the underlying contract itself would be impermissible (e.g. forward currency deals).","In a bilateral promise, ownership does not transfer at the time of promising, unlike in an actual 'Aqd.","Hamish Jiddiyah = token/earnest money taken before execution of the agreement, held in trust, adjusted into price at execution; actual (not opportunity-cost) loss recoverable on default.","'Arbun = part-payment taken after execution of the sale agreement, as part of the price.","Only the Hanbali school treats Bai' al-'Arbun as a valid independent sale contract; AAOIFI prefers refunding any 'Arbun beyond the seller's actual loss rather than full forfeiture."],"definitions":[{"term":"Wa'dah / 'Ahd","definition":"A promise -- does not create contractual rights and obligations by default, but may become binding, particularly where the promisee has relied on it."},{"term":"Hamish Jiddiyah","definition":"Earnest money/margin taken from a promisee before execution of an agreement, to confirm seriousness of intent to purchase; held as a trust and adjusted in the price at execution; actual loss (not cost of funds) is recoverable on default."},{"term":"'Arbun","definition":"Downpayment taken from the buyer as part of the price after execution of the sale agreement; the seller may (per Hanbali view) forfeit it if the buyer backs out."}],"conditions":["For a unilateral commercial promise to be binding: it must be unilateral, the promisee must have incurred liability/expense in reliance on it, the actual sale must occur via fresh offer and acceptance, and default remedies are limited to compelled performance or actual (not opportunity-cost) damages."],"principles":[],"processSteps":["Promisor makes a unilateral promise (e.g. to purchase an asset).","Bank/promisee takes Hamish Jiddiyah (token money) and, relying on the promise, incurs cost (e.g. purchases the asset).","If the promisor honors the promise, execution proceeds via fresh offer and acceptance and the token money is adjusted into the price.","If the promisor reneges, the bank recovers its actual loss from the Hamish Jiddiyah and returns any excess."],"examples":[{"title":"Car purchase promise default","kind":"textbook","text":"A asks bank B to buy a car and promises to buy it from B at $20,000. B purchases it for $18,000. A backs out; B resells it for $17,000. The net loss of $1,000 is borne by A, recoverable from his Hamish Jiddiyah."},{"title":"House promise breach","kind":"textbook","text":"A promises to sell a house to bank B for $100,000 next month, but sells it to C before the month elapses. A is liable to make good B's actual loss (e.g. costs incurred arranging to lease, sell, or use the house)."}],"commonConfusions":["Students often conflate Hamish Jiddiyah and 'Arbun -- the key distinction is timing: Hamish Jiddiyah is taken BEFORE the sale agreement is executed (still just a promise stage); 'Arbun is taken AFTER execution of the sale agreement, as part of the price.","A binding promise to purchase is not itself the sale -- the actual 'Aqd still needs a fresh offer and acceptance at the appointed time."],"distinctions":["Hamish Jiddiyah (pre-execution earnest money, actual-loss recovery) vs 'Arbun (post-execution part-price, potentially forfeitable in full per Hanbali view)","Wa'dah (unilateral promise, can become binding) vs 'Aqd (actual contract, ownership transfers immediately)"],"relatedConcepts":["Murabaha to Purchase Orderer","Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek","Diminishing Musharakah","Khiyar"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.6","pages":[114,115,116,117]}}],"conditions":["Unilateral promise","Promisor caused promisee to incur liabilities/expenses","Actual sale executed later by offer and acceptance","On breach: specific performance or actual damages (no opportunity cost)"],"debate":[{"issue":"Is a promise to purchase binding?","criticism":"Dylan Ray: medieval Fiqh sources indicate the promise to purchase should not be binding; modern practice creates conflict and objections to Murabaha as practised.","response":"Many traditional jurists (Malikis, Hanbalis, some Hanafis and Shafi‘is) and almost all contemporary jurists accept binding commercial promises; there is no contradiction of any Nass, so it is valid under Ibahatul Asliyah.","alternative":"A Maliki view: promises bind only where the promisee incurred expenses or liability on the basis of the promise.","takeaway":"In the mainstream view followed by the book, commercial promises are binding with actual-loss damages."},{"issue":"Is a promise to purchase binding?","criticism":"Dylan Ray argues that by medieval Fiqh the promise to purchase ought not to be binding, and treating it as binding creates conflict among scholars.","response":"The author says there is no conflict: many Maliki, Hanbali and some Hanafi and Shafi‘i jurists and almost all contemporary scholars accept binding commercial promises; it violates no text and is allowed under general permissibility; the OIC Fiqh Academy made it binding with conditions.","alternative":"Some Malikis make the promise binding only if the promisee incurred expense or liability in reliance on it.","takeaway":"Binding unilateral promises are accepted, but only actual loss (not opportunity cost) is recoverable."}],"calc":{"type":"promise-breach","note":"Reproduces the textbook’s car example: purchase cost, promised price, resale value → recoverable loss."}},{"id":"t5.6.1","section":"5.6.1","title":"Token Money (Hamish Jiddiyah) and ‘Arbun","pages":[116,117],"tier":"core","concepts":["hamish-jiddiyah","arbun"],"intuition":"A bank takes money upfront to show a client is serious. Is it a deposit, part of the price, or a forfeit?","simpleExplanation":"Hamish Jiddiyah is security money taken before the sale to show seriousness; the bank holds it in trust, adjusts it in the price, or deducts actual loss if the promise is broken and refunds the rest. ‘Arbun is earnest money paid after the sale is executed as part of the price; the seller may keep it if the buyer fails to perform, though AAOIFI prefers refunding the excess over actual loss.","academicExplanation":["Hamish Jiddiyah: taken before execution of the agreement to convey firm intention; held as a trust and adjusted in the price at execution. If the promisee fails to honour the promise, the bank recovers actual loss and returns the remainder; actual loss does not include cost of funds (AAOIFI). Tender deposits operate similarly: held in trust, refunded to unsuccessful bidders, part of price if accepted.","‘Arbun: earnest money paid at execution of the sale as part of the price. If the buyer fails to perform within the stipulated period, the seller may retain it; AAOIFI considers it preferable to refund what exceeds the loss actually sustained. Imam Malik defined ‘Arbun (a dinar given: counted in price if ratified, forfeited if cancelled) and considered it invalid; two Hadiths (for and against) are weak. Only the Hanbali school considers Bai‘ al ‘Arbun legal, relying on Naf‘i ibn al-Harith’s purchase of a prison house for Umar from Safwan. Contemporary majority: legal if the buyer freely stipulates finalising within set days or forfeiting the advance. But open options with no intention to buy, aimed at profit by risk transfer, contradict Islamic finance philosophy — conventional options are not accepted. ‘Arbun is acceptable as part payment after finalisation; its use to legitimise conventional options is generally not accepted."],"examExplanation":"Hamish Jiddiyah: before sale; trust; adjust in price or deduct actual loss and refund rest. ‘Arbun: after sale, part of price; forfeitable on buyer’s non-performance (AAOIFI: refund excess over actual loss). Only Hanbalis accept Bai‘ al ‘Arbun; not a basis for conventional options.","keyPoints":["Timing difference: before (HJ) vs after (‘Arbun) execution.","Cost of funds is not part of actual loss.","Conventional options not accepted."],"definitions":[{"term":"Hamish Jiddiyah","definition":"Token/security money taken before a sale to show the promisee’s firm intention; held in trust."},{"term":"‘Arbun","definition":"Earnest money paid at execution of a sale as part of the price, retainable by the seller if the buyer fails to perform."}],"examples":[{"title":"Bid money in tenders","kind":"textbook","text":"Bidders deposit part of the price; if the bid is accepted it becomes part of the price, otherwise it is returned as a trust (less actual damage) (p. 116)."},{"title":"Naf‘i and the Makkah prison house","kind":"textbook","text":"Naf‘i ibn al-Harith bought a prison house from Safwan for 4,000 dirhams, with 400 to go to Safwan if Umar did not approve — the Hanbali basis for ‘Arbun (pp. 116–117)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t6.9","t9.8.2","t8.8.8"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which statement is correct?","options":["‘Arbun is taken before the sale; HJ after","HJ is taken before the sale as a trust; ‘Arbun is part of price after execution","Both are interest","Both are always forfeited"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 116."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"distinctions":[{"a":"Hamish Jiddiyah","b":"‘Arbun","text":"HJ is taken before the sale as a trust against a promise; ‘Arbun is paid at execution of the sale as part of the price and may be forfeited on non-performance."}]},{"id":"t5.7","section":"5.7","title":"Types of Contracts: Valid Contracts (Sahih)","pages":[117,120],"tier":"core","concepts":["contract-validity"],"intuition":"Hanafis sort contracts into valid, voidable and void. What makes a contract valid — and can a valid contract still be suspended or revocable?","simpleExplanation":"Most jurists: valid (Sahih) or invalid (Batil). Hanafis add Fasid (voidable). A valid contract is correct in its essence (Asl) and attributes (Wasf). It may be effective immediately (Nafiz) or suspended (Mawquf) pending ratification, and binding (Lazim) or nonbinding (Ghair Lazim). Ijarah, Istisna‘a, Kafalah and Hawalah may take effect from a future date; a sale may not.","academicExplanation":["Most jurists divide contracts into valid (Sahih) and invalid (Batil); Hanafis into Sahih, Fasid and Batil. Zuhayli notes Hanafi texts often use ‘Fasid’ loosely. The book uses the three-way Hanafi scheme as it offers practitioners more options.","Valid contracts (5.7.1) conform in Asl (fundamental components) and Wasf (accessory attributes): form, subject matter and parties in order and free from Riba, Gharar etc. (Petty purchases by children and implied day-to-day transactions are exempt.) A valid contract is Nafiz (effective on execution) or Mawquf (suspended). Hanafis, Malikis and some Hanbalis allow delayed effect; Shafi‘is and some Hanbalis require immediate effect.","Future-effective contracts: Ijarah and Istisna‘a (usufruct owned gradually), Kafalah and Hawalah (e.g. “if your debtor has not paid by next month, I will”), agency, divorce, Waqf, bequest. Sale must be immediate — “I sell you this house at the beginning of next year” involves Gharar (lapse of interest, price changes, disputes — Siddiq al Dharir). Ibn al-Qayyim and Ibn Taymiyah allow ‘Aqd Mudhaf for sale too. Contemporary scholars suggest a unilateral promise instead.","Mawquf causes: defective capacity (a minor’s transaction subject to guardian ratification, retroactive if ratified); lack of authority (Fuduli); rights of a third party (selling a mortgaged house needs mortgagee ratification; the buyer may revoke before ratification, the seller may not).","Lazim (binding): no unilateral revocation unless Khiyar al-Shart. Ghair Lazim by nature: Wakalah, Kafalah, Shirkah, Wadi‘ah, ‘Ariyah — terminable by either party, unless they agree not to terminate for a period; hence banks can restrict early withdrawal of Shirkah-based investment deposits. Shareholders cannot terminate but can sell shares. Khiyar al-Shart prevents a contract becoming Lazim during the option."],"examExplanation":"Hanafi: Sahih, Fasid, Batil. Valid = Asl + Wasf in order. Nafiz vs Mawquf (minor, Fuduli, third-party rights). Lazim vs Ghair Lazim (Wakalah, Kafalah, Shirkah, Wadi‘ah, ‘Ariyah revocable by nature). Future effect OK for Ijarah, Istisna‘a, Kafalah, Hawalah, agency; not for sale (use a promise).","keyPoints":["Sale cannot be made effective from a future date (majority).","Mawquf contracts become effective on ratification (retroactively).","Investment deposits can be locked by agreement even though Shirkah is revocable by nature."],"definitions":[{"term":"Asl / Wasf","definition":"The fundamental components/essence of a contract / its accessory circumstances or external attributes."},{"term":"Nafiz / Mawquf","definition":"Immediately effective / suspended pending ratification or a future event."},{"term":"Lazim / Ghair Lazim","definition":"Binding (no unilateral revocation) / nonbinding (revocable by a party)."},{"term":"Fuduli","definition":"A person acting for another without authority (neither guardian nor agent, or an agent exceeding limits)."},{"term":"‘Aqd Mudhaf","definition":"A contract made effective from a future date."}],"examples":[{"title":"Surety from a future date","kind":"textbook","text":"“If your debtor has not paid you by the beginning of next month, I will pay” is a valid Kafalah effective from a future date; “I sell you this house at the beginning of next year” is not a valid sale (p. 118)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t5.7.2","t5.7.3","t12.7.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which contract can NOT be made effective from a future date according to the majority?","options":["Ijarah","Istisna‘a","Kafalah","Sale"],"correctAnswer":3,"explanation":"p. 118."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch5-t7"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch5-t7","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"5.7.1","sectionTitle":"Valid Contracts","title":"Valid (Sahih) Contracts: Nafiz and Mawquf","covers":["t5.7"],"overview":"A valid contract satisfies its 'Asl (essential elements) and Wasf (external attributes); it may be immediately effective (Nafiz) or suspended pending a condition (Mawquf).","simpleExplanation":"A contract is 'valid' when everything required is properly in place and it's free from things like interest or excessive uncertainty. Once valid, it usually takes effect right away -- but in a few situations (like a contract made by a minor, or by someone without proper authority, or touching a third party's rights) it's put on hold until someone confirms or cancels it. Contracts can also be 'binding' (nobody can back out alone) or 'non-binding' (either side can cancel), depending on the type of contract.","academicExplanation":"A valid contract conforms to Islamic law in both its 'Asl (fundamental components/essence) and Wasf (accessory/external attributes) -- form, subject matter and parties are all in order, conditions are met, and it is free from prohibited elements like Riba and Gharar. It becomes effective (Nafiz) upon execution unless suspended (Mawquf). Jurists (Hanafis, Malikis, some Hanbalis) hold that a valid contract's effect can be delayed to a future event (Mawquf), while Shafi'is and some Hanbalis require immediate effectiveness. Causes of suspension (Mawquf) include: (1) defective capacity, e.g. a minor's transaction with mixed benefit/harm, valid subject to later ratification by guardian or by the minor after puberty (ratification acts retrospectively; refusal voids it); (2) lack of proper authority -- a contract by a Fuduli (someone acting without proper agency authority), also subject to ratification; (3) a third party's right, e.g. a mortgagor selling mortgaged property is subject to the mortgagee's ratification, and the mortgagee bank can demand the debt be settled first. Contracts of Ijarah and Istisna'a may become effective from a future date (since usufruct is owned gradually, not instantly, as in sale); Kafalah and Hawalah are likewise effective from a future date. A sale contract, however, must be immediately effective per the majority (delay introduces Gharar over whether interest/consent will persist); Ibn al-Qayyim and Ibn Taymiyah dissent, permitting 'Aqd Mudhaf without distinguishing sale from lease. Valid contracts (Sahih and Nafiz) further split into Lazim (binding -- neither party may unilaterally revoke, absent a stipulated Khiyar al-Shart) and Ghair Lazim (nonbinding -- revocable unilaterally), the latter either because the contract type is inherently nonbinding (e.g. Wakalah, Kafalah, Shirkah, Wadi'ah, 'Ariyah -- though parties may mutually agree to make them irrevocable for a period) or because an option (Khiyar al-Shart) has been stipulated.","examExplanation":"Valid = 'Asl (essential elements) + Wasf (lawful external attributes, free of Riba/Gharar) both satisfied. Nafiz = immediately effective; Mawquf = suspended pending ratification (causes: minor's transaction, Fuduli's unauthorized act, third-party/mortgagee rights). Ijarah, Istisna'a, Kafalah, Hawalah can be effective from a future date; sale must be immediately effective (majority view) -- Ibn Taymiyah/Ibn al-Qayyim dissent. Lazim (binding, no unilateral revocation) vs Ghair Lazim (nonbinding, e.g. Wakalah/Kafalah/Shirkah/Wadi'ah/'Ariyah by nature, or any contract with a stipulated Khiyar al-Shart).","keyPoints":["A valid contract must satisfy both 'Asl (fundamental elements) and Wasf (external/nonessential attributes, e.g. freedom from Riba/Gharar).","Nafiz = immediately effective on execution; Mawquf = suspended until the cause of suspension (e.g. ratification) is resolved.","Mawquf causes: defective capacity (minor's contract), lack of authority (Fuduli), and a third party's right (e.g. mortgagee's claim).","Ijarah, Istisna'a, Kafalah, and Hawalah can validly be effective from a future date; sale generally must be immediately effective (majority view; Ibn Taymiyah/Ibn al-Qayyim dissent).","Lazim (binding) contracts cannot be unilaterally revoked absent a stipulated option; Ghair Lazim (nonbinding) contracts (e.g. Wakalah, Kafalah, Shirkah, Wadi'ah, 'Ariyah) can be revoked by either party unless parties agree otherwise for a period.","Khiyar al-Shart (option of stipulation) keeps a contract nonbinding for the holder of the option until the option period lapses.","Petty transactions by children (that create no rights/liabilities) and implied day-to-day transactions are exempt from strict Majlis/formality requirements."],"definitions":[{"term":"Nafiz","definition":"Immediately effective -- a valid contract whose elements are in order, conditions met, external attributes legal, and which is not suspended."},{"term":"Mawquf","definition":"A contract whose effectiveness is suspended until a future happening (e.g. ratification)."},{"term":"Fuduli","definition":"A person who is neither guardian nor agent, or who, if an agent, exceeds the limits prescribed by the principal."},{"term":"Lazim","definition":"A binding contract in which no party has a unilateral right to revoke without the other's consent (absent a stipulated option)."},{"term":"Ghair Lazim","definition":"A nonbinding contract in which either party may unilaterally revoke."}],"conditions":["For validity: form (offer/acceptance), parties, and subject matter must all be in order, and the contract must be free from Riba, Gharar and other prohibited elements.","A Mawquf contract requires ratification (by a guardian, the minor at majority, or the party with the relevant right) to become fully effective."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Sale of mortgaged property","kind":"textbook","text":"If A's house is mortgaged with a bank and A sells it without the bank's consent, the sale is a Mawquf contract subject to the bank's (mortgagee's) ratification; the bank will demand its debt be paid first."}],"commonConfusions":["Nafiz vs Mawquf is about timing of effect, not about validity itself -- a Mawquf contract is still 'Sahih' (valid in its elements), just suspended pending ratification.","Lazim/Ghair Lazim (binding/nonbinding, about revocability) is a separate axis from Nafiz/Mawquf (immediate/suspended effect) -- students sometimes conflate the two."],"distinctions":["Nafiz (immediately effective) vs Mawquf (suspended, pending ratification)","Lazim (binding, no unilateral revocation) vs Ghair Lazim (nonbinding, revocable unilaterally)"],"relatedConcepts":["Khiyar al-Shart","Fasid contracts","Batil contracts"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.1","pages":[117,118,119,120]}}],"subsections":[{"number":"5.7.1","title":"Valid Contracts","page":118,"points":["Asl and Wasf","Nafiz / Mawquf","Future-effective contracts","Lazim / Ghair Lazim"]}],"table":{"caption":"Contracts that may start from a future date (p. 118)","head":["May be effective from a future date","Must be effective immediately"],"rows":[["Ijarah, Istisna, Kafalah, Hawalah, agency, Waqf, bequest","Sale (Bai‘) — majority view"],["Reason: usufruct/obligation accrues over time","Reason: postponement adds Gharar"]]}},{"id":"t5.7.2","section":"5.7.2","title":"Voidable (Fasid) Contracts","pages":[120,123],"tier":"core","concepts":["contract-validity"],"intuition":"Can a defective contract be fixed? In Hanafi law, yes — if the defect is in the attributes, not the essence.","simpleExplanation":"A Fasid contract is legal in its essence (Asl) but defective in attributes (Wasf); remove the defect and it becomes valid. Causes include coercion (for Hanafis), missing information, and invalid conditions. If goods were delivered with consent, ownership passes and market value — not the agreed price — applies.","academicExplanation":["Causes of invalidity: intrinsic (basic elements — unlawful/nonexistent subject matter, lack of capacity) and extrinsic (Wasf — Riba, Gharar). Riba and Gharar are causes of irregularity in Hanafi law but of invalidity in other schools; even for Hanafis such contracts are unenforceable until the term is removed.","Factors: defective consent (coercion — Batil for the majority; Fasid/suspended subject to ratification for Hanafis); lack of value-relevant information about the subject matter (e.g. unidentified Ijarah asset — though Ijarah Mosufah bil Zimmah, a well-described service without identified units, is permissible), consideration (no definite price), time of performance (not for partnership, which is nonbinding by origin), or guarantee/pledge; invalid conditions — against the contract’s purpose (buyer may not resell), expressly prohibited (sale conditional on another sale/loan/gift), against commercial usage (seller to grind corn), or advantageous to one party (seller to reside in the sold house for two months). Irregular conditions affect only compensatory contracts; in gratuitous contracts (loan, gift, Waqf, Kafalah, mortgage, Hawalah, marriage) only the condition is abrogated.","In voidable Shirkah/Mudarabah, helpers receive matching wages while the capital provider claims the residual; jurists introduced Ribh-al-mithl to protect capital owners. Forms of Fasid sale (Hanafi): Bai‘ al-Majhul; contingent contract; sale effective from a future date; Bai‘ al-Ghaib (regularised by seeing, or valid if description suffices); unlawful consideration; two sales in one.","Legal status: must be revoked; no rights arise unless the defect is removed (e.g. a loan with an interest condition — the condition is void, the loan valid, principal only). If the buyer takes possession with the seller’s consent, ownership passes and the buyer pays agreed or market value; parties can still revoke unless the buyer has disposed of or altered it — an onward sale is valid. Differences from valid: ownership passes by possession (not mere offer/acceptance); market value (not agreed price) — market rent in voidable leases, profit in proportion to capital in voidable partnerships."],"examExplanation":"Fasid: Asl valid, Wasf defective; curable. Causes: coercion (Hanafi), Jahl about subject/price/time/security, invalid conditions (against purpose, expressly prohibited, against usage, one-sided advantage). Effects: ownership by consensual possession; market value/rent; profit per capital; onward sale valid.","keyPoints":["Irregular conditions void compensatory contracts but only drop out of gratuitous ones.","Ijarah Mosufah bil Zimmah is permissible.","Voidable partnership: profit in proportion to capital.","Onward sale by buyer in a Fasid sale is valid."],"definitions":[{"term":"Fasid","definition":"Voidable/defective contract — valid in essence (Asl) but defective in attributes (Wasf); can be regularised."},{"term":"Ijarah Mosufah bil Zimmah","definition":"Lease of a well-defined service/benefit without identifying particular units of the asset (e.g. transport by vans of a defined type)."},{"term":"Bai‘ al-Majhul","definition":"Sale in which the object, price or time of payment is unknown."}],"examples":[{"title":"Ijarah Mosufah bil Zimmah","kind":"textbook","text":"A bank hires air-conditioned vans of a defined type to carry staff; no particular van is identified, so destruction of one van does not end the lease — the lessor must supply another (p. 121, n. 37)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t5.7.3","t5.9","t4.2.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In a voidable (Fasid) partnership, how is profit allocated?","options":["As agreed","In proportion to each partner’s capital","Equally","All to the manager"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 123."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch5-t8"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch5-t8","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"5.7.2","sectionTitle":"Voidable (Fasid) Contracts","title":"Voidable (Fasid) Contracts","covers":["t5.7.2"],"overview":"A Fasid contract has all its essential elements ('Asl) correct but a defect in its external attributes (Wasf); it can be cured by removing the defect, unlike a Batil contract.","simpleExplanation":"A voidable contract isn't broken at its core -- it has an offer, acceptance, and real subject matter -- but something about it is technically wrong, like a missing detail or an unfair extra condition. Fix that one problem and the contract becomes fully valid. Until it's fixed, either side can cancel it.","academicExplanation":"A Fasid contract is legal in its 'Asl (has all elements of a contract) but illegal in its Wasf (external/nonessential attributes); it is not automatically void but can be regularized by removing the cause of irregularity. Causes of irregularity are intrinsic (relating to basic elements, e.g. unlawfulness/nonexistence of subject matter, lack of party capacity) or extrinsic (relating to Wasf, e.g. Riba or Gharar) -- notably, in Hanafi law, Riba and Gharar are treated as causes of irregularity (Fasid) rather than invalidity (Batil), though even under Hanafi law such a contract is unenforceable until the offending term is removed. Major factors rendering a contract Fasid include: defective consent (coercion -- treated by Hanafis as Fasid/curable by ratification, versus the majority who treat it as Batil); lack of value-relevant information (Gharar/Jahl) about the subject matter, consideration, time of performance, or guarantee/security; and invalid collateral conditions -- e.g. conditions against the contract's purpose (buyer barred from reselling), expressly Shari'ah-prohibited conditions (tying the sale to an unrelated loan or gift), conditions against commercial usage, or conditions benefiting one party at the other's cost. These irregular-condition rules affect only compensatory (commutative) contracts, not gratuitous ones (loan, gift, Waqf) or suretyship-type contracts (Kafalah, mortgage, Hawalah, marriage) -- in the latter, only the offending condition is struck, not the whole contract. Hanafi-identified forms of Fasid contracts include Bai' al-Majhul (unknown subject/price/payment time), contingent sale, a sale contract with delayed effect, Bai' al-Gha'ib (unseen item, curable by inspection or adequate description), sale with unlawful consideration, and 'two sales in one' (one commodity, two unstipulated prices). Legal status: a Fasid contract must be revoked (no rights/obligations arise) unless the defect is cured, in which case it becomes valid. If a buyer takes possession with the seller's consent before revocation, ownership passes at market value (or mutually agreed value), not the originally stipulated price -- distinguishing Fasid contracts from valid ones (where ownership transfers by mere offer/acceptance at the agreed price). Once the buyer has disposed of, altered, or added to/subtracted from the item so it's no longer the same object, neither party can annul it; a subsequent onward sale by the buyer remains valid and enforceable.","examExplanation":"Fasid = valid 'Asl (elements present) but defective Wasf (external attribute) -- curable by removing the defect. Causes: intrinsic (unlawful/nonexistent subject, incapacity) or extrinsic (Riba/Gharar -- in Hanafi law treated as Fasid, not Batil). Fasid forms: Bai' al-Majhul, contingent sale, future-effective sale, Bai' al-Gha'ib, unlawful consideration, two-sales-in-one. Irregular-condition rule applies only to compensatory contracts (not to loan/gift/Waqf/Kafalah/Hawalah/marriage, where only the bad condition is struck). Key distinguishing rule: in Fasid sale with buyer possession, ownership passes at MARKET value, not the originally agreed price (unlike valid contracts, where the agreed price governs).","keyPoints":["Fasid = correct 'Asl (essential elements) but defective Wasf (external attributes); curable by removing the defect, unlike Batil (void ab initio).","Intrinsic causes of Fasid relate to basic elements (unlawful/nonexistent subject, lack of capacity); extrinsic causes relate to Wasf (Riba, Gharar).","In Hanafi law, Riba and Gharar make a contract Fasid (irregular, curable), not automatically Batil -- but it remains unenforceable until the offending term is removed.","Named Fasid forms: Bai' al-Majhul, contingent sale, sale effective from a future date, Bai' al-Gha'ib, sale with unlawful consideration, two sales in one.","Invalid/irregular conditions (against contract purpose, expressly prohibited, against usage, or one-sided benefit) can make a compensatory contract Fasid; in gratuitous/suretyship contracts, only the bad condition is struck, the contract itself survives.","If the Fasid-contract buyer takes possession with the seller's consent, ownership passes -- but at market value, not the originally agreed price, until revoked.","Once the buyer has disposed of, altered, or transformed the goods, the contract can no longer be annulled by either party; a subsequent onward sale remains valid."],"definitions":[{"term":"Fasid","definition":"A voidable or defective contract (per Hanafi Fiqh) due to nonfulfilment of a condition required for full validity -- curable by removing the defect."},{"term":"Bai' al-Majhul","definition":"A sale in which the object, price, or time of payment remains unknown and unspecified."},{"term":"Bai' al-Gha'ib","definition":"Sale of absent or concealed goods, not visible/inspected at the time of contracting, curable by later inspection or a satisfactory prior description."}],"conditions":["A Fasid contract becomes valid once its specific defect (e.g. missing information, an invalid condition) is removed.","Invalid conditions void only themselves in noncompensatory/suretyship contracts (loan, gift, Waqf, Kafalah, mortgage, Hawalah, marriage) -- the underlying contract survives."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Interest condition in a loan","kind":"textbook","text":"If a lender inserts an interest condition into a loan contract, the interest condition is void and, once removed, the loan contract is valid -- the debtor owes only the principal."},{"title":"Fasid sale, buyer takes possession","kind":"textbook","text":"If the buyer takes an item with the seller's consent in a Fasid sale (e.g. subject matter not originally identified but later mutually identified), ownership passes at market value or a mutually agreed value, not necessarily the price fixed in the original agreement."}],"commonConfusions":["Fasid is often confused with Batil; the key distinction is that Fasid contracts can be cured by removing the defect and, once possession occurs with consent, ownership passes (at market value) -- Batil contracts never pass ownership and goods must be returned.","Students may think any invalid condition voids the whole contract; for gratuitous/suretyship-type contracts, only the invalid condition itself is struck."],"distinctions":["Fasid (curable, ownership can pass at market value with consented possession) vs Batil (void, no ownership ever transfers, goods must be returned)","Compensatory contracts (irregular condition can make the whole contract Fasid) vs gratuitous/suretyship contracts (only the bad condition falls away)"],"relatedConcepts":["Batil contracts","Valid (Sahih) contracts","Riba","Gharar"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.2","pages":[120,121,122,123]}}],"table":{"caption":"Valid vs voidable contract effects","head":["Aspect","Valid (Sahih)","Voidable (Fasid)"],"rows":[["Ownership passes by","Offer and acceptance","Possession taken with seller’s consent"],["Price/rent","Agreed price/rent","Market value / market rent"],["Partnership profit","Agreed ratio","In proportion to capital"],["Cure","—","Remove the cause of irregularity"]]}},{"id":"t5.7.3","section":"5.7.3","title":"Void (Batil) Contracts","pages":[123,124],"tier":"core","concepts":["contract-validity"],"intuition":"What if a contract fails at its root — no valid offer, parties or deliverable subject?","simpleExplanation":"A Batil contract fails major conditions (form, capacity, subject matter, possession/delivery) or has illegal attributes. It has no effect: no ownership passes, goods must be returned, and the original seller can reclaim goods even from a third party.","academicExplanation":["Contracts not fulfilling conditions of offer and acceptance, subject matter, consideration and possession/delivery, or with illegal external attributes, are Batil. Examples: milk in the udder; unknown consideration or period; a dirham for two dirhams; bidding over a bid after parties agree; contracts actuated by fraud. Permissible forms include Salam, auction bidding, Bai‘ al Khiyar, Musawamah and Murabaha. A Batil contract gives rise to no effect: no title to goods or price; delivered goods must be returned whether or not the illegality was known; if the buyer sells to a third party, the original seller can still claim the goods — unlike a Fasid contract."],"examExplanation":"Batil: fails major conditions → no effect; goods returned; original seller can reclaim even from third party (unlike Fasid).","keyPoints":["Auction bidding is permitted; bidding over an agreed bid is not."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Void vs voidable on resale","kind":"textbook","text":"If goods delivered under a Batil contract are resold, the original seller can still reclaim them because ownership never passed; after a Fasid sale with consent, an onward sale is valid and cannot be undone (pp. 123–124)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Bidding against other bidders is always prohibited.","right":"Bidding over another’s bid after two parties have agreed a price is prohibited; open competitive bidding at auction is permissible (Imam Malik, p. 124 n. 44)."}],"relatedTopics":["t5.7.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In a Batil sale, the buyer has already resold the goods to a third party. The original seller:","options":["Loses all rights","Can still claim the goods, since ownership never passed","Must accept market value","Must pay the third party"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 124."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch5-t9"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch5-t9","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"5.7.3","sectionTitle":"Void (Batil) Contracts","title":"Void (Batil) Contracts","covers":["t5.7.3"],"overview":"A Batil contract fails to fulfil basic conditions on offer/acceptance, subject matter, consideration, or possession, or involves illegal external attributes, and produces no legal effect at all.","simpleExplanation":"A void contract is fundamentally broken from the start -- it never creates any ownership or rights for anyone, no matter what happens afterward. If goods were handed over under a void deal, they have to be given back, even if both sides didn't realize it was invalid.","academicExplanation":"A Batil contract fails to satisfy major conditions of offer/acceptance, subject matter, consideration, or possession/deliverability, or otherwise involves illegal external attributes. Examples given include a sale with absolute uncertainty/speculation (e.g. selling milk still in a cow's udder), sale with unknown consideration and unknown time period, sale of a dirham for two dirhams, bidding over an already-agreed price, and contracts actuated by fraud or deceit. By contrast, permissible forms of Bai' include Salam, sale by bidding, Bai' al-Khiyar, Musawamah, and Murabaha. A Batil contract produces no legal effect whatsoever: the buyer gains no title to the subject matter, the seller gains no title to the price, ownership never transfers, and the transaction is null and void. If delivery has already occurred, the goods must be returned to the original owner regardless of whether the illegality was known to the parties; even if the buyer has already sold the goods to a third party, the original seller can still claim them back, since ownership was never transferred through a Batil contract. This is the key legal distinction (Hukm) from a Fasid contract, where consented possession can pass ownership at market value and a subsequent onward sale by the buyer remains valid.","examExplanation":"Batil = fails major conditions (offer/acceptance, subject matter, consideration, possession) or has illegal attributes -- produces NO legal effect: no ownership transfer ever, for either party. Goods delivered under a Batil contract must be returned (even from a third-party buyer, since ownership was never validly transferred), unlike Fasid contracts where consented possession can pass ownership (at market value) and onward sales by the buyer remain valid.","keyPoints":["Batil = failure of major conditions (offer/acceptance, subject matter, consideration, possession/delivery) or illegal external attributes.","Examples: sale of milk in a cow's udder (absolute uncertainty), sale with unknown consideration/time, sale of a dirham for two dirhams, bidding over an already-concluded price, contracts by fraud/deceit.","A Batil contract produces no legal effect at all -- no title to subject matter or price ever passes; ownership never transfers.","Goods delivered under a Batil contract must be returned to the original owner, regardless of whether the parties knew of the illegality.","Even if the buyer has resold the goods under a Batil contract to a third party, the original seller can still reclaim them, because ownership never validly transferred.","This total non-transfer of ownership is the key distinction (Hukm) from a Fasid contract, where consented possession can pass ownership at market value."],"definitions":[{"term":"Batil","definition":"A void or invalid contract that does not fulfil conditions relating to offer and acceptance, subject matter, consideration, or possession/delivery, or which involves illegal external attributes like Riba, Gharar, or Qimar."}],"conditions":[],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Third-party sale under a Batil contract","kind":"textbook","text":"If a buyer under a Batil contract takes delivery and then sells the goods to a third party, the original seller can still claim the goods back, since ownership was never validly transferred through the Batil contract."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes assume a good-faith third-party buyer is protected; the text is explicit that the original seller can reclaim goods from a third party in a Batil chain because ownership was never transferred in the first place."],"distinctions":["Batil (void, no ownership ever transfers, goods always reclaimable) vs Fasid (voidable, curable, ownership can pass at market value on consented possession)"],"relatedConcepts":["Fasid contracts","Valid (Sahih) contracts","Gharar","Riba"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.3","pages":[123,124]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Fasid","b":"Batil","text":"Fasid can be cured and consensual possession transfers ownership (onward sale valid); Batil has no effect at all and goods can be reclaimed even from third parties."}]},{"id":"t5.8","section":"5.8","title":"Commutative and Noncommutative Contracts","pages":[124,125],"tier":"core","concepts":["commutative-contracts","wakalah","kafalah","hawalah","qard"],"intuition":"Which contracts can carry a charge — and can a bank charge for a guarantee?","simpleExplanation":"Commutative contracts (sale, lease, Wakalah) can carry compensation; gratuitous ones (loan, gift, guarantee, Hawalah, Waqf, bequest, ‘Ariyah) cannot. Banks may charge for services via Wakalah or Ju‘alah (e.g. on L/Cs and guarantees) based on expenses — amount-based, not time-based. A void condition voids a commutative contract but merely drops out of a gratuitous one.","academicExplanation":["Uqood-e-Mu‘awadha (commutative): sale, purchase, lease, Wakalah. Sales by object: Muqayadhah (barter), Bai‘ al Hal (spot), Sarf, Salam, Mu’ajjal (credit), Mutlaq (absolute). By price: Tawliyah (at cost), Murabaha (cost plus profit), Wadhi‘ah (at a loss) — together Buyu’al Amanat (trust sales) — and Musawamah (bargaining without reference to cost). Ijarah: Ijarat al Ashkhas (services) and Ijarat al Ashya (things). Istisna‘a. Wakalah can be commutative or not.","Uqood Ghair Mu‘awadha (Tabarru‘): Hibah, Wasiyyah, Waqf, Kafalah, ‘Ariyah, Qard, Hawalah. Kafalah, Qard and Hawalah are relevant to banks, but no profit may be charged on them per se; fees for other services on Wakalah or Ju‘alah are allowed (e.g. issuing L/Cs and guarantees), based on expenses — amount-based (possibly slabs), not time-based.","Legal status: a void condition voids a commutative contract; in a gratuitous contract only the condition is ineffective (an interest condition in a loan is void; the loan remains). Gharar does not invalidate gratuitous contracts (donating a stray animal is fine; selling it is not)."],"examExplanation":"Commutative: sale (Muqayadhah, Hal, Sarf, Salam, Mu’ajjal, Mutlaq; Tawliyah, Murabaha, Wadhi‘ah = trust sales; Musawamah), Ijarah, Istisna‘a, Wakalah. Gratuitous: Hibah, Wasiyyah, Waqf, Kafalah, ‘Ariyah, Qard, Hawalah — no charge; service fees via Wakalah/Ju‘alah, amount-based not time-based.","keyPoints":["Trust sales (Buyu’al Amanat): Tawliyah, Murabaha, Wadhi‘ah.","Guarantee fees: based on expenses; amount-based, not time-based.","Gharar tolerated in gratuitous contracts."],"definitions":[{"term":"Buyu’al Amanat (trust sales)","definition":"Bai‘ Tawliyah (at cost), Bai‘ Murabaha (cost plus profit) and Bai‘ Wadhi‘ah (at a loss) — sales referring to the original cost."},{"term":"Bai‘ Musawamah","definition":"Sale by bargaining without reference to the original cost price."},{"term":"‘Ariyah","definition":"Loan of a usable item free of any charge."}],"examples":[{"title":"Fee on a guarantee","kind":"textbook","text":"A bank issuing a letter of guarantee may not charge for the guarantee itself, since Kafalah is gratuitous. It may recover charges for related services as an agent, based on its expenses and the amount involved, not on the time outstanding (pp. 124–125)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t9.11","t13.2","t14.6","t1.8"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"How may an Islamic bank charge for issuing a guarantee, according to Section 5.8.2?","options":["A time-based percentage like interest","Service charges based on expenses, amount-based but not time-based","No charge at all ever","A share of the client’s profit"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 125."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch5-t10"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch5-t10","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"5.8","sectionTitle":"Commutative and Noncommutative Contracts","title":"Commutative (Mu'awadha) vs Noncommutative (Tabarru') Contracts","covers":["t5.8"],"overview":"Contracts split into Uqood-e-Mu'awadha (commutative, remunerative) and Uqood Ghair Mu'awadha/Tabarru' (noncommutative, gratuitous), with different legal consequences for void conditions.","simpleExplanation":"Some contracts are about exchanging value for value, like buying, selling, or renting -- you can legitimately earn a return from these. Others are about giving without getting anything back, like a loan, a gift, or a guarantee -- Islamic banks can't legally charge extra just for these, only fees for related services they actually perform.","academicExplanation":"Contracts split by consideration/counter-value into Uqood-e-Mu'awadha (compensatory/commutative -- sale, hire, Wakalah, etc., where one party legitimately earns remuneration) and Uqood Ghair Mu'awadha/Tabarru' (noncommutative/gratuitous -- loan/Qard, gift/Hibah, guarantee/Kafalah, assignment of debt/Hawalah -- where no return/compensation is permissible). Within commutative contracts, sale (Bai') subdivides by object (Bai' Muqayadhah/barter, Bai' al-Hal/spot, Bai' al-Sarf/currency exchange, Bai' Salam, Bai' Mu'ajjal/deferred payment, Bai' Mutlaq/normal sale) and by price basis (Bai' Tawliyah/resale at cost, Bai' Murabaha/cost-plus, Bai' Wadhi'ah/resale at a loss -- together called Buyu' al-Amanat or 'trust sales' -- and Bai' Musawamah/bargained price with no cost reference). Ijarah splits into Ijarat al-Ashkhas (rendering services) and Ijarat al-Ashya (letting things); Istisna'a is manufacturing-on-order; Wakalah can be either commutative or noncommutative. Among Tabarru' contracts: Hibah, Wasiyyah, Waqf, Kafalah, 'Ariyah, Qard, and Hawalah -- of these, Kafalah, Qard, and Hawalah are directly relevant to Islamic banking, but banks cannot charge any profit against these contracts per se; they can only charge fees for genuinely separate services on a Wakalah or Ju'alah basis (e.g. amount-based, not time-based, fees for issuing L/Cs or guarantees). Legally, commutative contracts become void upon insertion of a void condition, whereas noncompensatory/Tabarru' contracts do not become void because of a void condition -- only the offending condition itself becomes ineffective (e.g. an interest-based loan: the interest condition is void, but the loan contract itself remains effective and the principal remains due). Similarly, Gharar does not invalidate noncompensatory contracts -- e.g. donation of a stray or unidentified animal is permissible even though its sale would not be valid.","examExplanation":"Mu'awadha (commutative) = sale, hire, Wakalah -- return/profit is legitimate. Tabarru' (noncommutative/gratuitous) = Qard, Hibah, Kafalah, Hawalah, 'Ariyah, Waqf, Wasiyyah -- no return/profit allowed on the contract itself; banks may only charge separate service fees (Wakalah/Ju'alah basis, amount-based not time-based). Key legal-effect rule: a void condition voids a commutative contract, but in a noncompensatory (Tabarru') contract only the void condition itself falls away -- the contract (e.g. a loan) remains valid and enforceable for its principal.","keyPoints":["Mu'awadha (commutative) contracts allow legitimate compensation: sale (Bai'), hire (Ijarah), Wakalah (can be either), Istisna'a.","Tabarru' (noncommutative/gratuitous) contracts do not allow compensation: Qard (loan), Hibah (gift), Wasiyyah (bequest), Waqf, Kafalah (guarantee), 'Ariyah, Hawalah (assignment of debt).","Sale (Bai') subdivides by object (barter, spot, currency, Salam, deferred, normal) and by price basis (cost-only/Tawliyah, cost-plus/Murabaha, loss/Wadhi'ah -- together 'trust sales'; plus Musawamah).","Kafalah, Qard, and Hawalah are directly relevant to Islamic banking; banks cannot charge profit against these per se, only genuine service fees under Wakalah/Ju'alah, and such fees should be amount-based (e.g. slabs), not time-based.","A void condition voids a commutative contract, but in a noncompensatory (Tabarru') contract only the void condition falls away -- the underlying contract stays valid.","Gharar does not invalidate noncompensatory contracts (e.g. donating a stray/unidentified animal is fine, though selling it would not be)."],"definitions":[{"term":"Uqood-e-Mu'awadha","definition":"Commutative contracts in which one can genuinely take a return, such as Bai', Ijarah and Wakalah."},{"term":"Uqood Ghair Mu'awadha (Tabarru')","definition":"Noncommutative/gratuitous contracts in which one cannot get any return or compensation, such as loan (Qard), gift (Hibah/Tabarru'), guarantee (Kafalah), and assignment of debt (Hawalah)."},{"term":"Buyu' al-Amanat","definition":"Fiduciary/'trust' sales -- Murabaha, Tawliyah and Wadhi'ah -- where the seller declares the cost price to the buyer."}],"conditions":["Banks may not charge profit/return directly on Tabarru' contracts (Qard, Kafalah, Hawalah) themselves; only amount-based service fees for genuinely separate services (Wakalah/Ju'alah) are allowed."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Void interest condition in a loan","kind":"textbook","text":"A person enters an interest-based loan; the interest condition is void, but the loan contract remains effective and the debtor must repay the loan/debt (principal) as it falls due."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think a void condition always kills the whole contract; the rule differs by contract type -- commutative contracts become void with a void condition, but Tabarru' contracts merely drop the bad condition and remain valid.","Banks charging for Kafalah/guarantees must be careful the fee is for a genuine separate service (fixed/amount-based), not disguised interest on the guarantee itself, and must not be time-based."],"distinctions":["Mu'awadha (commutative, compensation allowed) vs Tabarru' (noncommutative, no compensation on the contract itself, only separate service fees)","Effect of a void condition: voids the whole contract (commutative) vs voids only the condition (Tabarru')"],"relatedConcepts":["Qard","Kafalah","Hawalah","Wakalah","Ju'alah","Murabaha"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.8","pages":[124,125,126]}}],"subsections":[{"number":"5.8.1","title":"Uqood-e-Mu‘awadha","page":124,"points":["Sale by object and by price","Ijarah types","Istisna‘a"]},{"number":"5.8.2","title":"Uqood Ghair Mu‘awadha (Tabarru‘)","page":125,"points":["Seven gratuitous contracts","Fee rules for L/C and guarantees"]},{"number":"5.8.3","title":"Legal Status","page":125,"points":["Void condition: voids commutative; ineffective in gratuitous"]}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Commutative contract with a void condition","b":"Gratuitous contract with a void condition","text":"A void condition voids a sale or lease; in a loan, gift or guarantee only the condition falls away — an interest clause is struck out and the loan stands (p. 125)."}],"table":{"caption":"Classification of contracts (Section 5.8)","head":["Commutative (Mu‘awadha)","Gratuitous (Ghair Mu‘awadha / Tabarru‘)"],"rows":[["Sale (all forms)","Hibah (gift)"],["Ijarah (services / things)","Wasiyyah (bequest)"],["Istisna‘a","Waqf (endowment)"],["Wakalah (can be either)","Kafalah (guarantee)"],["—","‘Ariyah (free loan of usable item)"],["—","Qard (loan)"],["—","Hawalah (assignment of debt)"]]}},{"id":"t5.9","section":"5.9","title":"Conditional or Contingent Contracts","pages":[126,127],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["contract-validity"],"intuition":"Can you add conditions to a sale — warranties, free service, a pledge? Some conditions are fine, some void.","simpleExplanation":"Conditions are of three kinds: suspending (T‘aliq), deferring (Idafa) and concomitant (Iqtiran). A condition that supports the contract’s purpose or is customary (warranty, free service, pledge) is valid. A condition against the purpose (never resell) or harmful is void. If an invalid condition is added to an otherwise valid contract, the condition drops and the contract stands (per the sources cited).","academicExplanation":["Conditional contracts are generally not valid, but some conditions are acceptable. Three types: T‘aliq (suspends to a future event), Idafa (delays the beginning), Iqtiran (concomitance varying terms). Hanafis and Hanbalis allow delayed beginning for lease or agency but not sale. All schools consider whether a condition agrees with the contract’s purpose: paying the price, transferring full title, paying in certain currency, or providing a pledge are valid; ‘never resell’ is not. Conditions giving one party extra benefit are disputed; Ibn Taymiyah rejects only those contradicting the Qur’an, Sunnah, Ijma‘a or the contract’s object. Hanbalis emphasise party discretion; Hanafis, Shafi‘is and Malikis classify conditions as valid, irregular and void. Valid conditions include Khiyar al-Shart and Khiyar al-Ru’yah, withholding goods until payment, a pledge for the price and customary conditions (Jaber’s camel ride). A Fasid condition in an otherwise valid contract is void while the contract is enforceable; a superfluous condition is unenforceable; a condition repugnant to the contract but benefiting one party, if inseparable, vitiates it. Market-standard conditions such as a five-year guarantee, free service, warranties and cancellation options are valid."],"examExplanation":"T‘aliq, Idafa, Iqtiran. Valid: conditions consistent with contract purpose or customary (pledge, warranty, free service, Khiyar). Void: against purpose, contradict text, harmful. Ibn Taymiyah’s practical approach.","keyPoints":["Warranty and after-sales service conditions are valid.","“Buyer will never resell” is invalid."],"definitions":[{"term":"T‘aliq / Idafa / Iqtiran","definition":"Suspension to a future event / deferral of the start / concomitant condition varying terms."}],"examples":[{"title":"Valid customary conditions","kind":"textbook","text":"Conditions like a five-year guarantee, one year’s free service, a warranty, repair terms or a pledge for the price are valid because they support the contract or are customary (pp. 126–127)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Any condition in a sale makes it invalid.","right":"Only conditions that contradict the texts, harm a party or defeat the contract’s purpose are void; Ibn Taymiyah rejects only those conflicting with the Qur’an, Sunnah, Ijma‘a or the object of the contract (p. 126)."}],"relatedTopics":["t5.7.2","t6.8"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which condition in a sale is valid according to Section 5.9?","options":["The buyer will never resell the item","The seller provides a five-year guarantee and one year’s free service","The buyer must lend the seller money","The seller will live in the sold house for two months"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 127."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch5-t11"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch5-t11","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"5.9","sectionTitle":"Conditional or Contingent Contracts","title":"Conditional or Contingent Contracts: T'aliq, Idafa, Iqtiran","covers":["t5.9"],"overview":"As a general rule conditional contracts are not valid, but the Fiqh literature distinguishes three types of stipulation (T'aliq, Idafa, Iqtiran) and evaluates each condition against the purpose of the contract.","simpleExplanation":"You generally can't make a contract depend on some future 'if.' But not every added condition is a problem -- some conditions just support the deal (like requiring payment before handover), some are standard market practice, and those are fine. What's not fine is a condition that contradicts the whole point of the contract, breaks Islamic law directly, or unfairly benefits one side without reason.","academicExplanation":"As a general rule, conditional contracts are not valid, though this needs nuance. Fiqh literature distinguishes three types of stipulation: T'aliq (conditions suspending a contract on a future event), Idafa (an extension delaying the start of a contract to a future time), and Iqtiran (concomitance -- a condition that varies the contract's terms). In all three, the contract may or may not be void even where the condition itself is void; jurists differ on the outcome. Hanafis and Hanbalis permit delay in beginning contracts like lease or agency (where property/usufruct transfers gradually over time), but not for sale. Regarding concomitant conditions, schools examine whether a condition agrees with or conflicts with the contract's purpose: stipulations that the buyer pay the price, or that the seller transfer full title, or fix payment in a certain currency, or require a pledge as security, are all valid; a condition barring the buyer from ever reselling is not approved. Hanbali jurists give the widest latitude, upholding every condition/stipulation not contradicting explicit Qur'an/Sunnah text; Hanafi, Shafi'i and Maliki jurists instead sort conditions into valid, irregular and void. Valid conditions confirm effects the Shari'ah already attributes to the act and are explicitly admitted by it -- e.g. Khiyar al-Shart (option of stipulation) and Khiyar al-Ru'yah (option of inspection), or a stipulation that goods won't be handed over until price is paid. A condition 'in aid of' the contract (e.g. requiring security/pledge for a credit price) is likewise valid, as is any condition that has become customary market practice, provided it doesn't contradict explicit Shari'ah text. A void condition is one that directly infringes a Shari'ah rule, harms one of the contracting parties, or derogates from completing the contract -- but per the general rule for Fasid contracts, a void condition inserted into an otherwise valid contract voids only the condition, leaving the contract itself valid and enforceable without regard to it. A condition of no advantage to either party is superfluous and unenforceable; a condition repugnant to the contract's purpose but advantageous to one party can make the transaction Fasid if inseparable from it.","examExplanation":"General rule: conditional contracts are not valid, with nuance. Three stipulation types: T'aliq (suspend to future event), Idafa (delay start date), Iqtiran (vary terms). Delay-of-start permitted for Ijarah/Wakalah (gradual transfer), not for sale (must be immediate). Conditions sorted as valid (support the contract's purpose or Shari'ah-sanctioned, e.g. Khiyar al-Shart, security pledge, market-customary terms), irregular/void (contradicts contract purpose, expressly Shari'ah-prohibited, against usage, or unfairly favors one party). A void condition in an otherwise valid contract voids only the condition, not the whole contract (consistent with the Fasid-contract rule).","keyPoints":["General rule: conditional/contingent contracts are not valid, but valid stipulations exist depending on type and purpose.","Three stipulation types: T'aliq (suspends contract to a future event), Idafa (delays start of contract), Iqtiran (varies terms concomitantly).","Delayed start is permitted for Ijarah and Wakalah (gradual transfer of usufruct/agency), but sale must be immediately effective per the majority.","Hanbali jurists uphold any condition not contradicting explicit Qur'an/Sunnah text; Hanafi/Shafi'i/Maliki jurists classify conditions as valid, irregular, or void.","Valid conditions: those Shari'ah already sanctions (Khiyar al-Shart, Khiyar al-Ru'yah), those aiding the contract's fulfilment (e.g. pledge as security, payment-before-handover), and customary market terms.","Void conditions: those directly infringing Shari'ah, harming a party, or derogating from completing the contract.","A void condition inserted into an otherwise valid contract voids only the condition -- the contract itself remains valid and enforceable without it."],"definitions":[{"term":"T'aliq","definition":"Conditions which suspend a contract to any future event."},{"term":"Idafa","definition":"An extension that delays the beginning of any contract until a future time."},{"term":"Iqtiran","definition":"Concomitance -- a condition that varies the terms of the contract."},{"term":"Khiyar al-Shart","definition":"A right, stipulated by one or both parties, to cancel the contract for any reason within a fixed period of time."}],"conditions":["A condition is valid if it confirms Shari'ah-sanctioned effects, aids the contract's purpose, or reflects customary market practice not contradicting explicit text.","A condition is void if it directly infringes a Shari'ah rule, harms a contracting party, or derogates from completing the contract's core purpose."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Warranty condition is valid","kind":"textbook","text":"A condition that the seller will provide five years' guarantee and one year's free service is not void, nor is a warranty against defective goods a problem, since these support rather than contradict the sale's purpose."},{"title":"Camel purchase with a ride condition","kind":"textbook","text":"Jaber bought a camel from the holy Prophet with a condition of a ride home included in the contract -- an example of a valid, contract-supporting condition upheld in the Hadith literature."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think any added condition beyond the bare price/subject matter is automatically invalid; the text is clear that many collateral conditions (security, warranty, currency of payment) are valid as long as they don't contradict the contract's core purpose or explicit Shari'ah text.","A void condition does not always void the whole contract -- in an otherwise valid contract, only the offending condition is struck (consistent with the Fasid-contract cure rule from 5.7.2)."],"distinctions":["T'aliq (suspends on future event) vs Idafa (delays start date) vs Iqtiran (varies terms)","Conditions permitted in Ijarah/Wakalah (delayed start allowed) vs sale (must be immediate per majority)"],"relatedConcepts":["Khiyar al-Shart","Fasid contracts","Mawquf contracts"],"examRelevance":"supporting","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.9","pages":[126,127]}}]}],"questions":[{"id":"q5.1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which type of goods is suitable for Salam according to Section 5.2?","options":["Qimi (non-fungible) goods","Mithli (fungible) goods","Only real estate","Currencies"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 103.","topicId":"t5.2","chapter":5,"section":"5.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Classify goods","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.2","pages":[101,103]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.2","type":"tf","prompt":"An Ijarah contract that transfers ownership of the asset to the lessee as an automatic effect of the lease is void.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"p. 102.","topicId":"t5.2","chapter":5,"section":"5.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Apply ownership rules to Ijarah","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.2","pages":[101,103]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.3","type":"match","prompt":"Match the term to its meaning.","pairs":[["Sighah","Offer and acceptance"],["‘Aqidain","Contracting parties"],["Ma‘qud ‘alayh","Subject matter"],["Majlis","Contractual session"]],"explanation":"Sections 5.4 and 5.4.1.","topicId":"t5.4","chapter":5,"section":"5.4","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify elements of a contract","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.4","pages":[106,106]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.4","type":"scenario","prompt":"A seller says: ‘Take this item; if you pay within a month it is $100, within two months $105,’ and the buyer agrees without choosing. The contract is:","options":["Valid","Invalid because the price is uncertain","Valid if the buyer pays in one month","A Salam"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 109.","topicId":"t5.4.2","chapter":5,"section":"5.4.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply price-certainty rule","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.4.2","pages":[108,110]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.5","type":"multi","prompt":"Which contract combinations does Section 5.5.5 describe as permissible (subject to conditions)? (Select all.)","options":["Shirkah with Ijarah","Sale with a loan in one contract","Wakalah with Murabaha","Salam with Murabaha for export finance","One article for two unchosen prices"],"correctAnswer":[0,2,3],"explanation":"p. 112–113.","topicId":"t5.5.5","chapter":5,"section":"5.5.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Identify permissible combinations","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5.5","pages":[112,113]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.6","type":"application","prompt":"A client promises to buy a car the bank purchased for $18,000 at $20,000, then backs out. The bank resells for $17,000. How much can the bank recover?","options":["$3,000","$2,000","$1,000","$0"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"Actual loss = 18,000 − 17,000 = $1,000; expected profit is not recoverable (p. 114).","topicId":"t5.6","chapter":5,"section":"5.6","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Compute actual loss on promise breach","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.6","pages":[114,116]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.7","type":"comparison","prompt":"Which statement correctly contrasts Hamish Jiddiyah and ‘Arbun?","options":["Both are taken after the sale","HJ is taken before sale and held in trust; ‘Arbun is part of the price at execution","‘Arbun is always refunded","HJ is part of the price"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 116.","topicId":"t5.6.1","chapter":5,"section":"5.6.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish HJ and ‘Arbun","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.6.1","pages":[116,117]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.8","type":"identify","prompt":"A contract valid in its essence (Asl) but defective in its external attributes (Wasf), which can be regularised by removing the defect, is:","options":["Batil","Fasid","Nafiz","Lazim"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 120.","topicId":"t5.7.2","chapter":5,"section":"5.7.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Classify contract validity","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.2","pages":[120,123]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.9","type":"mcq","prompt":"In a voidable lease contract, the lessor is entitled to:","options":["The rent specified in the agreement","Equitable market rent","Nothing","Double rent"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 123.","topicId":"t5.7.2","chapter":5,"section":"5.7.2","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain effects of Fasid contracts","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.2","pages":[120,123]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.10","type":"tf","prompt":"A condition of interest inserted into a loan makes the entire loan contract void.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Loans are gratuitous; the condition is void but the loan remains, repayable at principal (pp. 123, 125).","topicId":"t5.8","chapter":5,"section":"5.8","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply effect of void conditions","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.8","pages":[124,125]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.11","type":"definition","prompt":"‘Resale at cost price plus profit — bargaining on profit margin’ is:","options":["Bai‘ Tawliyah","Bai‘ Wadhi‘ah","Bai‘ Murabaha","Bai‘ Musawamah"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"p. 124.","topicId":"t5.8","chapter":5,"section":"5.8","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Classify sales by price","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.8","pages":[124,125]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.12","type":"order","prompt":"Order the steps of forming a contract as described in Section 5.4.","items":["Offer (Ijab) made clearly","Acceptance (Qabul) conforming to the offer","Both in the same session (or within granted time)","Formal event concludes the contract; ownership and risk transfer"],"explanation":"Sections 5.4.1–5.4.2.","topicId":"t5.4.1","chapter":5,"section":"5.4.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Sequence contract formation","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.4.1","pages":[106,108]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.13","type":"scenario","prompt":"A homeowner whose house is mortgaged to a bank signs a contract to sell it without the bank’s involvement. The contract is:","options":["Batil","Mawquf (suspended) pending the mortgagee’s ratification","Fully valid and effective","Fasid due to Riba"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 119.","topicId":"t5.7","chapter":5,"section":"5.7","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Identify suspended contracts","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7","pages":[117,120]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.14","type":"short","prompt":"Why is a sale contract not allowed to take effect from a future date, while Ijarah can?","keywords":["usufruct","gradually","Gharar","promise"],"correctAnswer":"Usufruct is owned gradually over time, so Ijarah (and Istisna‘a) can start from a future date. A sale transfers ownership immediately; postponing it involves Gharar because a party’s interest may lapse or the market/object may change, leading to regret and disputes. Contemporary scholars suggest a unilateral promise instead (Ibn Taymiyah and Ibn al-Qayyim differ).","explanation":"Section 5.7.1.","topicId":"t5.7","chapter":5,"section":"5.7","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Explain future-effective contracts","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7","pages":[117,120]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.15","type":"tf","prompt":"Fiduciary money is a kind of Mal, but it cannot itself be the subject matter of sale.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"It serves as medium of exchange and measure of value (p. 101).","topicId":"t5.2","chapter":5,"section":"5.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the status of money as Mal","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.2","pages":[101,103]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.16","type":"match","prompt":"Match each type of ownership to its Arabic term.","pairs":[["Ownership of the asset itself","Milk ul‘Ain"],["Ownership of a debt","Milk ud Dayn"],["Ownership of usufruct","Milk ul Manf‘at"]],"explanation":"p. 102.","topicId":"t5.2","chapter":5,"section":"5.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall categories of ownership","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.2","pages":[101,103]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.17","type":"identify","prompt":"Which term refers to a covenant with more sanctity than an ordinary contract, used for treaties and marriage?","options":["‘Aqd","Mithaq","Wa‘dah","Ijab"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 103.","topicId":"t5.2.1","chapter":5,"section":"5.2.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Distinguish contractual terms","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.2.1","pages":[103,105]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.18","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the governing principle for transactions (Mu‘amalat), according to Ibn Taymiyah as quoted in Chapter 5?","options":["Everything is prohibited unless commanded","Permissibility, unless prohibited by Allah and His Prophet","Only what jurists approve","Custom always prevails"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 105.","topicId":"t5.3","chapter":5,"section":"5.3","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall general permissibility","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.3","pages":[105,106]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.19","type":"tf","prompt":"In a commercial contract, a party can escape his obligation by proving that his intention (Niyyah) differed from the words he signed.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"In Mu‘amalat the apparent wording has legal effect (p. 105).","topicId":"t5.3","chapter":5,"section":"5.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Apply the form-over-intention rule","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.3","pages":[105,106]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.20","type":"multi","prompt":"In which cases is an offer cancelled? (Select all.)","options":["The maker withdraws it","A party dies or loses capacity","The session ends without a contract","The subject matter is destroyed","The buyer asks a question about quality"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2,3],"explanation":"Also lapse of time fixed for acceptance (p. 107).","topicId":"t5.4.1","chapter":5,"section":"5.4.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall when offers lapse","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.4.1","pages":[106,108]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.21","type":"comparison","prompt":"How do the Council of Islamic Ideology and the Federal Shariat Court of Pakistan differ on Ijab?","options":["They agree fully","The CII says only an act (not abstention) forms Ijab; the FSC says a contract may be to do or to abstain from doing something","The FSC rejects written contracts","The CII allows only oral offers"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 106.","topicId":"t5.4.1","chapter":5,"section":"5.4.1","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Compare views on the offer","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.4.1","pages":[106,108]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.22","type":"scenario","prompt":"A house mortgaged to a bank is sold by its owner without the bank’s consent. What is the status of the sale?","options":["Void","Mawquf — suspended pending the mortgagee’s ratification","Fully valid","Fasid"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Right of a third party suspends the contract (p. 119).","topicId":"t5.7","chapter":5,"section":"5.7","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Identify a suspended contract","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7","pages":[117,120]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.23","type":"application","prompt":"A seller says: “Take this and I will tell you the price later.” The buyer agrees. The contract is:","options":["Valid","Not valid — the price must be agreed and fixed at execution","Valid if the price is fair","A Salam"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 109.","topicId":"t5.4.2","chapter":5,"section":"5.4.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the certainty-of-price rule","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.4.2","pages":[108,110]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.24","type":"mcq","prompt":"According to Section 5.4.2, what are the two ways of determining the subject matter?","options":["By oath or witness","By inspection at the contract, or by description (with specific identification if similar units exist)","By price and weight","By custom or court"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 109.","topicId":"t5.4.2","chapter":5,"section":"5.4.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall determination of subject matter","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.4.2","pages":[108,110]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.25","type":"identify","prompt":"Hakim ibn Hizam wanted to sell goods he had bought before taking possession. Which Caliph stopped him?","options":["Abu Bakr","Umar ibn al-Khattab","Uthman","Ali"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 109.","topicId":"t5.4.2","chapter":5,"section":"5.4.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the possession precedent","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.4.2","pages":[108,110]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.26","type":"tf","prompt":"Speculation per se — buying with an eye to future price changes — is prohibited in Islamic law.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Only sales of non-existent/unowned goods and gambling are prohibited (p. 111).","topicId":"t5.5","chapter":5,"section":"5.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish speculation from Gharar","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5","pages":[110,114]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.27","type":"application","prompt":"A trader wants to sell a bill receivable at a discount to a bank. What does Chapter 5 say?","options":["Allowed at any discount","Not allowed; transferring it under Hawalah at face value with recourse to the original debtor is valid","Allowed only for exporters","Allowed if the bank is Islamic"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 111.","topicId":"t5.5","chapter":5,"section":"5.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the rule on sale of debt","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5","pages":[110,114]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.28","type":"order","prompt":"Order the four forms of prohibited mutually contingent contracts as listed in Section 5.5.5.","items":["Sale of two articles where buying one obliges buying the other","One article for two prices without finalising one","Contingent sale","Combining sale and lending"],"explanation":"p. 112.","topicId":"t5.5.5","chapter":5,"section":"5.5.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall prohibited combinations","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5.5","pages":[112,113]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.29","type":"scenario","prompt":"In an Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek, the contract makes the lessee bear the risk of total destruction of the asset during the lease. What is the effect?","options":["Valid — the lessee has use","It renders the transaction void; ownership risk must stay with the lessor","It converts it into a sale","It is Makruh only"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 112.","topicId":"t5.5.5","chapter":5,"section":"5.5.5","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply risk allocation in Ijarah","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5.5","pages":[112,113]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.30","type":"short","prompt":"Why must the price in Salam be paid in full in advance?","keywords":["debt for a debt","delay","both sides","prepayment"],"correctAnswer":"Because an exchange with delay on both sides is an exchange of a debt for a debt (liability for liability), which is prohibited. In Salam the goods are deferred, so the price must be paid immediately.","explanation":"Ibn Rushd’s nine kinds of exchange (p. 113).","topicId":"t5.5.5","chapter":5,"section":"5.5.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the prepayment requirement in Salam","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5.5","pages":[112,113]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.31","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why is a creditor not entitled to any excess over the principal, according to the principle of “profits with liability”?","options":["Because loans are short-term","Because he bears no business risk on the amount lent","Because the State forbids it","Because inflation is low"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 113.","topicId":"t5.5.7","chapter":5,"section":"5.5.7","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain profit with liability","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5.7","pages":[113,113]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.32","type":"multi","prompt":"Which conditions did the OIC Fiqh Academy set for a binding promise in commercial dealings? (Select all.)","options":["The promise must be unilateral","The promisor must have caused the promisee to incur liabilities or expenses","The sale itself must later occur by offer and acceptance","Damages include opportunity cost","On breach, the court may compel purchase or actual damages"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2,4],"explanation":"Opportunity cost is excluded (p. 115).","topicId":"t5.6","chapter":5,"section":"5.6","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall conditions of binding promises","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.6","pages":[114,116]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.33","type":"comparison","prompt":"What is the key difference between a bilateral promise (Muwa‘adah) and a contract (‘Aqd)?","options":["There is no difference","Ownership does not pass when a promise is signed; under ‘Aqd ownership passes and inheritance rules apply at once","A promise is always oral","A contract cannot be binding"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Majority: bilateral promises are not allowed where the ‘Aqd itself is not allowed (pp. 115–116).","topicId":"t5.6","chapter":5,"section":"5.6","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Distinguish promise from contract","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.6","pages":[114,116]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.34","type":"application","prompt":"A client gave Hamish Jiddiyah of 2,000 and then refused to buy. The bank’s actual loss was 800. What happens?","options":["The bank keeps all 2,000","The bank recovers 800 and returns 1,200","The bank keeps 2,000 plus cost of funds","Nothing is returned"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Actual loss only, excluding cost of funds; the excess is returned (p. 116).","topicId":"t5.6.1","chapter":5,"section":"5.6.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply Hamish Jiddiyah rules","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.6.1","pages":[116,117]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.35","type":"identify","prompt":"Which school of Fiqh regards Bai‘ al ‘Arbun as a legal contract?","options":["Hanafi","Maliki","Shafi‘i","Hanbali"],"correctAnswer":3,"explanation":"Based on the report of Naf‘i ibn al-Harith (p. 116).","topicId":"t5.6.1","chapter":5,"section":"5.6.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the Hanbali view on ‘Arbun","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.6.1","pages":[116,117]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.36","type":"tf","prompt":"Conventional options are accepted in Islamic finance by analogy with ‘Arbun.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"‘Arbun is accepted only as part payment in a finalised deal; open options for transferring risk are not (p. 117).","topicId":"t5.6.1","chapter":5,"section":"5.6.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Evaluate options and ‘Arbun","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.6.1","pages":[116,117]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.37","type":"multi","prompt":"Which contracts are Ghair Lazim (revocable) by nature? (Select all.)","options":["Wakalah","Kafalah","Shirkah","Wadi‘ah","Sale"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2,3],"explanation":"Sale is binding (Lazim) once concluded (p. 119).","topicId":"t5.7","chapter":5,"section":"5.7","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Classify binding and non-binding contracts","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7","pages":[117,120]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.38","type":"scenario","prompt":"An Islamic bank’s investment deposit is based on Shirkah, which is revocable by nature. Can the bank stop early withdrawal?","options":["No, never","Yes — by an agreed clause in the account-opening form restricting withdrawal before the settled date","Only by court order","Only for large deposits"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 120.","topicId":"t5.7","chapter":5,"section":"5.7","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply mutual agreement on revocable contracts","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7","pages":[117,120]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.39","type":"identify","prompt":"Under Hanafi law, a contract made under coercion is:","options":["Batil","Fasid — capable of ratification","Sahih","Mawquf forever"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"The majority regard it as Batil (p. 120).","topicId":"t5.7.2","chapter":5,"section":"5.7.2","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the status of coerced contracts","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.2","pages":[120,123]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.40","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of these are invalid conditions in a sale? (Select all.)","options":["The buyer shall never resell the asset","The buyer of corn requires the seller to grind it","The seller keeps living in the sold house for two months","The buyer provides a pledge for the price"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2],"explanation":"A pledge supports the contract and is valid (pp. 121, 126).","topicId":"t5.7.2","chapter":5,"section":"5.7.2","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Identify invalid conditions","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.2","pages":[120,123]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.41","type":"mcq","prompt":"In a Fasid partnership, how is profit divided?","options":["As per the agreement","In proportion to each partner’s capital","Equally","All to the managing partner"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 123.","topicId":"t5.7.2","chapter":5,"section":"5.7.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall effects of Fasid partnership","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.2","pages":[120,123]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.42","type":"tf","prompt":"Donating a stray animal or fruit before its benefit is evident is permissible, although selling it is not.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"Gharar does not invalidate gratuitous contracts (p. 125).","topicId":"t5.8","chapter":5,"section":"5.8","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Apply Gharar to gratuitous contracts","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.8","pages":[124,125]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.43","type":"match","prompt":"Match each sale to its basis of classification.","pairs":[["Bai‘ Muqayadhah","Barter"],["Bai‘ al Sarf","Exchange of money"],["Bai‘ Tawliyah","Resale at cost"],["Bai‘ Wadhi‘ah","Resale at a loss"]],"explanation":"pp. 124–125.","topicId":"t5.8","chapter":5,"section":"5.8","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Classify sales by object and price","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.8","pages":[124,125]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q5.44","type":"identify","prompt":"Which type of stipulation delays the beginning of a contract until a future time?","options":["T‘aliq","Idafa","Iqtiran","Khiyar"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"T‘aliq suspends on a future event; Iqtiran varies terms (p. 126).","topicId":"t5.9","chapter":5,"section":"5.9","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Distinguish types of stipulations","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.9","pages":[126,127]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q-ch5-1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which of the following best describes 'Ain in Islamic commercial law?","explanation":"'Ain is a specific or determinate type of Mal (property), as distinguished from Dayn, which is nonspecific/indeterminate.","topicId":"t5.2","chapter":5,"section":"5.2","topicLabel":"Mal, Usufruct and Ownership","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Distinguish Mal, usufruct and ownership, and explain the 'Ain/Dayn and Mithli/Qimi classifications of property.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.2","pages":[101,102]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["A nonspecific obligation settled by any equivalent units of a kind of property","A determinate or specific type of property","The price paid in an exchange transaction","A gratuitous loan of a usable item"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch5-2","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: Getting ownership of the usufruct (Manf'at) of an asset also gives ownership of the asset itself.","explanation":"False. Ownership of 'Ain (the asset) carries ownership of its usufruct, but the reverse is not true -- getting usufruct (as in Ijarah) does not transfer ownership of the underlying asset.","topicId":"t5.2","chapter":5,"section":"5.2","topicLabel":"Mal, Usufruct and Ownership","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish Mal, usufruct and ownership, and explain the 'Ain/Dayn and Mithli/Qimi classifications of property.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.2","pages":[101]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch5-3","type":"short","prompt":"An Islamic bank wants to structure a forward-purchase contract for a specified quantity of wheat of a known standard grade. Should this use Salam (for Mithli goods) or Istisna'a (for Qimi goods), and why?","explanation":"The text states Salam is conducted for Mithli goods while Istisna'a is used for Qimi goods, and wheat of a given variety is a classic Mithli example.","topicId":"t5.2","chapter":5,"section":"5.2","topicLabel":"Mal, Usufruct and Ownership","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Distinguish Mal, usufruct and ownership, and explain the 'Ain/Dayn and Mithli/Qimi classifications of property.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.2","pages":[102,103]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Salam, because wheat of a particular standard grade is Mithli (fungible) -- all units of the same grade are interchangeable and priced identically, which is the category Salam is used for; Istisna'a is used instead for Qimi (nonfungible, made-to-order) goods."},{"id":"q-ch5-4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Per Ibn Taymiyah's distinction cited in the chapter, what is the governing rule for Mu'amalat (transactions)?","explanation":"For Mu'amalat, the governing principle is permissibility: nothing can be prohibited unless proscribed by Allah and His Prophet -- unlike Ibadat, where only what is commanded is obligatory.","topicId":"t5.3","chapter":5,"section":"5.3","topicLabel":"Ibadat vs Mu'amalat","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain the broad rules governing the validity of Mu'amalat.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.3","pages":[105]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Only what is expressly commanded is valid","Permissibility and absence of prohibition -- nothing is prohibited unless proscribed by the Qur'an and Sunnah","Intent (Niyyah) alone determines validity, regardless of wording","Every transaction requires prior scholarly authorization"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch5-5","type":"scenario","prompt":"A party to a Mu'amalat contract later claims the written terms don't reflect what he truly intended. According to the chapter, how would Islamic law treat this claim?","explanation":"The chapter states that in Mu'amalat, the factor considered is the apparent wording/format of the contract; a party cannot say it was not his intent. The law enforces what was actually agreed. (Niyyah is decisive only in Ibadat.)","topicId":"t5.3","chapter":5,"section":"5.3","topicLabel":"Ibadat vs Mu'amalat","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain the broad rules governing the validity of Mu'amalat.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.3","pages":[105]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["The claim succeeds because Niyyah (intent) always overrides written terms","The claim fails -- in Mu'amalat, the apparent wording/form of the contract has legal effect, not unexpressed intent","The contract is automatically void for Gharar","The claim succeeds only if the party is a minor"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch5-6","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of the following are among the three essential elements of a valid 'Aqd according to Muslim jurists?","explanation":"The three essential elements are the form (offer and acceptance), the contracting parties, and the subject matter. A written document is not itself a required element -- offer and acceptance can be conveyed by words, gesture, or conduct.","topicId":"t5.4","chapter":5,"section":"5.4","topicLabel":"Elements of a Contract","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"List the elements and components of a valid contract and the requirements for a proper offer and acceptance.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.4","pages":[106]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Sighah (form: offer and acceptance)","'Aqidain (the contracting parties)","Ma'qud 'alayh (the subject matter)","A written document signed by both parties"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2]},{"id":"q-ch5-7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which of the following contract types is exempt from the requirement of 'unity of session' for offer and acceptance?","explanation":"The chapter notes that the requirement of unity of session does not apply to contracts of agency, gift, and appointment of an executor for a minor's property.","topicId":"t5.4.1","chapter":5,"section":"5.4.1","topicLabel":"Offer and Acceptance","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"List the elements and components of a valid contract and the requirements for a proper offer and acceptance.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.4.1","pages":[108]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Sale (Bai')","Agency (Wakalah)","Ijarah","Salam"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch5-8","type":"scenario","prompt":"A trading firm wants to buy a receivable (a debt owed to another company) at a discount, purely as a standalone sale of the debt. Based on Chapter 5, is this valid, and what would make it valid?","explanation":"The chapter states that sale of a debt/receivable is not valid per se because the seller doesn't know whether/when the debtor will pay (Gharar); discounting of bills is thus not allowed. However, subjecting it to the rules of Hawalah (assignment of debt) with recourse to the original debtor removes the Gharar and makes it valid.","topicId":"t5.4.2","chapter":5,"section":"5.4.2","topicLabel":"Elements of the Subject Matter","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"List the elements and components of a valid contract and the requirements for a proper offer and acceptance.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.4.2","pages":[108,111]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Valid as-is, because debts are Dayn and Dayn can always be traded like Mithli goods","Not valid as a standalone sale of debt (void for Gharar, since the debtor's payment is uncertain), but could be validated by structuring it under Hawalah rules with recourse to the original debtor","Valid as-is, because discounting bills is always permitted under Bai' al-Sarf","Not valid under any structuring, because all debt sales are Batil regardless of form"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch5-9","type":"short","prompt":"Define constructive possession (Qabza Hukmi).","explanation":"This is the book's definition of Hukmi (constructive) possession, relevant because the seller must have possession (physical or constructive) before reselling goods.","topicId":"t5.4.2","chapter":5,"section":"5.4.2","topicLabel":"Elements of the Subject Matter","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"List the elements and components of a valid contract and the requirements for a proper offer and acceptance.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.4.2","pages":[110]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"definition","correctAnswer":"A situation where the buyer has not taken physical delivery of the commodity, yet the commodity has come into his risk and control, and all rights and liabilities (including risk of destruction) have passed to him."},{"id":"q-ch5-10","type":"mcq","prompt":"Match each prohibited practice to its correct description: (1) Najash, (2) Ghaban-e-Fahish, (3) Talaqqi-al-Rukban -- with descriptions: (a) charging an exorbitant price while implying it's the market price, (b) bidding up a price with no intention to buy, (c) a city dweller exploiting a Bedouin's ignorance of market prices before he reaches town.","explanation":"Najash = false bidding with no purchase intent (b); Ghaban-e-Fahish = exorbitant price disguised as market price (a); Talaqqi-al-Rukban = exploiting a rural seller's price ignorance before he reaches market (c).","topicId":"t5.5","chapter":5,"section":"5.5","topicLabel":"Free Consent, Gharar, Riba, Qimar/Maisir","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the broad rules governing the validity of Mu'amalat.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5.1","pages":[110]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"matching","options":["1-b, 2-a, 3-c","1-a, 2-b, 3-c","1-c, 2-b, 3-a","1-b, 2-c, 3-a"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch5-11","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which two contract types are the sole Shari'ah-sanctioned exceptions to the rule that the sold commodity must exist and be in the seller's possession at the time of sale?","explanation":"Salam and Istisna'a are the only two exceptions to the existence/possession rule, validated by conditions (precise quality, quantity, price, delivery time/place) that remove Gharar.","topicId":"t5.5","chapter":5,"section":"5.5.2","topicLabel":"Free Consent, Gharar, Riba, Qimar/Maisir","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the broad rules governing the validity of Mu'amalat.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5.2","pages":[111]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Murabaha and Musawamah","Salam and Istisna'a","Ijarah and Wakalah","Kafalah and Hawalah"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch5-12","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: According to the chapter, speculation about future price movements is itself prohibited in Islamic commercial law.","explanation":"False. The chapter explicitly states speculation per se (buying/selling with an eye to possible future price changes) is not prohibited; what's prohibited is sale of nonexistent/unowned goods and transactions amounting to Maisir/Qimar.","topicId":"t5.5","chapter":5,"section":"5.5.2","topicLabel":"Free Consent, Gharar, Riba, Qimar/Maisir","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the broad rules governing the validity of Mu'amalat.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5.2","pages":[111]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch5-13","type":"short","prompt":"Differentiate Qimar from Maisir as defined in the chapter.","explanation":"Both are prohibited under the games-of-chance rule, but Qimar centers on chance-contingent possession while Maisir centers on unearned/effortless gain.","topicId":"t5.5","chapter":5,"section":"5.5.4","topicLabel":"Free Consent, Gharar, Riba, Qimar/Maisir","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain the broad rules governing the validity of Mu'amalat.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5.4","pages":[112]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Qimar is a game of chance/gambling where possession of property is contingent on an uncertain event; Maisir means getting something too easily, or a profit without working for it."},{"id":"q-ch5-14","type":"scenario","prompt":"An Islamic bank structures Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek so that ownership of the leased asset transfers to the lessee automatically as a built-in condition of the lease itself. Is this valid?","explanation":"The chapter states Bai' and Ijarah have totally different impacts (ownership/risk transfer in Bai' but not Ijarah) and must be kept as separate agreements; transferring ownership risk to the lessee during the lease as an automatic/binding feature would render the transaction void. Only a unilateral, nonbinding promise to sell/buy/gift at lease end is permitted.","topicId":"t5.5.5","chapter":5,"section":"5.5.5","topicLabel":"Contingent Contract Combinations","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain the broad rules governing the validity of Mu'amalat.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5.5","pages":[112,113]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Yes, because combining sale and lease is always allowed for Islamic banks","No, because sale and lease must be kept as separate contracts; ownership/risk transfer can only occur via a separate unilateral, nonbinding promise, not as a built-in condition of the lease","Yes, provided the lessee pays an extra fee","No, because Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek is prohibited outright"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch5-15","type":"short","prompt":"State the principle of profit-with-liability (Al-Ghunm bil-Ghurm) and its implication for interest on loans.","explanation":"This principle underlies the prohibition of Riba across the book: profit must be earned through risk-bearing, not through a risk-free claim on repayment.","topicId":"t5.5.7","chapter":5,"section":"5.5.7","topicLabel":"Profit with Liability","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the broad rules governing the validity of Mu'amalat.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5.7","pages":[113]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"definition","correctAnswer":"A person is entitled to profit only when he bears the risk of loss in business (Al-Ghunm bil-Ghurm). Any excess a debtor pays a creditor above the principal is prohibited because the creditor bears no business risk on the amount lent -- this is the basis for prohibiting interest."},{"id":"q-ch5-16","type":"mcq","prompt":"Under the OIC Islamic Fiqh Academy's conditions cited in the chapter, when does a unilateral promise in a commercial dealing become binding?","explanation":"The OIC Fiqh Academy conditions require the promise to be unilateral and that the promisor caused the promisee to incur liabilities/expenses; the actual sale must still occur via fresh offer and acceptance at the appointed time.","topicId":"t5.6","chapter":5,"section":"5.6","topicLabel":"Wa'dah (Promise)","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Describe the status of Wa'dah in Islamic law and when it becomes binding.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.6","pages":[115]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Whenever any promise is made, regardless of reliance","Only when it is unilateral and the promisee has been caused to incur liabilities or expenses in reliance on it","Only if it is put in writing and notarized","Never -- promises are inherently unenforceable in Islamic law"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch5-17","type":"scenario","prompt":"A asks Bank B to purchase a car and promises to buy it from B at $20,000. B purchases the car for $18,000. A then backs out of the promise, and B resells the car for $17,000. Under the rules discussed in the chapter, what can B recover from A?","explanation":"This matches the worked example in the text: the net loss ($18,000 cost - $17,000 resale = $1,000) is what A must bear, recoverable from his token money (Hamish Jiddiyah) -- not the full promised price or a hypothetical profit.","topicId":"t5.6","chapter":5,"section":"5.6","topicLabel":"Wa'dah (Promise)","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Describe the status of Wa'dah in Islamic law and when it becomes binding.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.6","pages":[114]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Nothing, since promises are never enforceable","The full $20,000 promised price","B's actual loss of $1,000 (the difference between B's cost of $18,000 and resale of $17,000), recoverable from A's Hamish Jiddiyah","B's expected profit of $2,000 (the difference between the promised price and B's cost)"],"correctAnswer":2},{"id":"q-ch5-18","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the key timing difference between Hamish Jiddiyah and 'Arbun?","explanation":"Hamish Jiddiyah is taken before the execution of an agreement to convey the promisee's seriousness; 'Arbun is taken from the buyer as part of the price after execution of the sale agreement.","topicId":"t5.6.1","chapter":5,"section":"5.6.1","topicLabel":"Hamish Jiddiyah and 'Arbun","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Describe the status of Wa'dah in Islamic law and the roles of Hamish Jiddiyah and 'Arbun.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.6.1","pages":[116]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Hamish Jiddiyah is taken after the sale is executed; 'Arbun is taken before","Hamish Jiddiyah is taken before execution of the sale agreement (still at the promise stage); 'Arbun is taken after execution of the sale agreement, as part of the price","There is no timing difference; the terms are interchangeable","Hamish Jiddiyah applies only to Salam contracts; 'Arbun applies only to Istisna'a"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch5-19","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: All four major Sunni schools of Fiqh regard Bai' al-'Arbun as a valid, independent sale contract.","explanation":"False. Among the main schools, only the Hanbali school considers Bai' al-'Arbun a legal/valid contract, relying on a report involving Naf'i Ibnal Harith; Imam Malik, for instance, considered such a deal invalid.","topicId":"t5.6.1","chapter":5,"section":"5.6.1","topicLabel":"Hamish Jiddiyah and 'Arbun","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Describe the status of Wa'dah in Islamic law and the roles of Hamish Jiddiyah and 'Arbun.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.6.1","pages":[116]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch5-20","type":"mcq","prompt":"A contract by a minor whose transaction carries both possible benefit and harm is:","explanation":"The chapter lists defective capacity (e.g. a minor's transaction with mixed benefit/harm) as a cause of a Mawquf contract -- suspended pending ratification, which acts retrospectively if granted, or voids the contract if refused.","topicId":"t5.7","chapter":5,"section":"5.7.1","topicLabel":"Valid Contracts","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Differentiate valid, voidable and void contracts.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.1","pages":[119]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Automatically void (Batil)","Automatically valid and binding regardless of guardian consent","A Mawquf (suspended) contract, valid subject to later ratification by the guardian or by the minor himself after puberty","Automatically Fasid and unfixable"],"correctAnswer":2},{"id":"q-ch5-21","type":"mcq","prompt":"According to the majority of jurists cited in the chapter, which contract type must be immediately effective and cannot be validly delayed to a future date?","explanation":"The contract of sale is immediately enforceable in the opinion of the majority of jurists -- 'I sell you this house at the beginning of next year' is not permissible, since delay introduces Gharar. Ijarah, Istisna'a, Kafalah and Hawalah, by contrast, can validly be effective from a future date.","topicId":"t5.7","chapter":5,"section":"5.7.1","topicLabel":"Valid Contracts","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Differentiate valid, voidable and void contracts.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.1","pages":[118]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Ijarah","Istisna'a","Sale (Bai')","Kafalah"],"correctAnswer":2},{"id":"q-ch5-22","type":"scenario","prompt":"A lender inserts an interest condition into an otherwise properly formed loan (Qard) contract. What is the legal status of the arrangement, per the rules discussed?","explanation":"Noncommutative contracts (like a loan) do not become void due to a void condition -- only the condition itself is removed. Once the interest condition is removed, the loan contract is valid and the debtor owes only the principal.","topicId":"t5.7.2","chapter":5,"section":"5.7.2","topicLabel":"Voidable (Fasid) Contracts","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Differentiate valid, voidable and void contracts.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.2","pages":[123]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["The entire loan contract is void (Batil), and no repayment obligation exists","The interest condition is void, but the loan contract remains effective -- the debtor must repay only the principal","The interest condition is valid because it was agreed by both parties","The whole arrangement becomes a Mawquf contract awaiting ratification"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch5-23","type":"short","prompt":"A sale contract is agreed where the object of sale, its price, and the time of payment all remain unknown and unspecified. Identify this specific Fasid form named in the chapter.","explanation":"Bai' al-Majhul is defined in the text as a sale in which the object of sale, its price, or the time of payment remains unknown and unspecified -- one of the Hanafi-identified forms of Fasid (voidable) contracts.","topicId":"t5.7.2","chapter":5,"section":"5.7.2","topicLabel":"Voidable (Fasid) Contracts","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Differentiate valid, voidable and void contracts.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.2","pages":[122]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"identify","correctAnswer":"Bai' al-Majhul"},{"id":"q-ch5-24","type":"scenario","prompt":"Goods are delivered under a Batil (void) contract. The buyer then resells them to an unsuspecting third party. Can the original seller reclaim the goods from the third party?","explanation":"The chapter states that in a Batil contract, ownership never transfers; if the buyer sells to a third party after taking delivery, the original seller cannot be prevented from claiming the goods, because ownership cannot be transferred through a Batil contract.","topicId":"t5.7.3","chapter":5,"section":"5.7.3","topicLabel":"Void (Batil) Contracts","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Differentiate valid, voidable and void contracts.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.3","pages":[124]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["No, the third party is a protected good-faith buyer","Yes, because ownership never validly transferred under the Batil contract, so neither the original buyer nor his third-party buyer ever obtained title","Only if the third party knew of the illegality","No, unless a court specifically orders it within 30 days"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch5-25","type":"comparison","prompt":"What is the key legal distinction (Hukm) between a Fasid contract and a Batil contract regarding possession taken with the seller's consent?","explanation":"The chapter explicitly distinguishes the two: in a Fasid sale, if the buyer takes possession with the seller's consent, ownership passes (at market value, not the originally stipulated price); in a Batil contract, no such effect occurs -- ownership never transfers at all.","topicId":"t5.7.3","chapter":5,"section":"5.7.3","topicLabel":"Void (Batil) Contracts","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Differentiate valid, voidable and void contracts.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.3","pages":[123,124]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["There is no distinction; both transfer ownership identically","In a Fasid contract, consented possession can pass ownership at market value; in a Batil contract, ownership never transfers regardless of possession","In a Batil contract, ownership passes at market value; in a Fasid contract it never transfers","Both contracts require court ratification before any effect"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch5-26","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of the following are classified as Uqood Ghair Mu'awadha (noncommutative/Tabarru' contracts) in the chapter?","explanation":"Qard, Hibah and Kafalah are listed as Tabarru' (noncommutative/gratuitous) contracts, along with Waqf, 'Ariyah and Hawalah. Bai' and Ijarah are commutative (Mu'awadha) contracts.","topicId":"t5.8","chapter":5,"section":"5.8","topicLabel":"Commutative vs Noncommutative Contracts","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Distinguish commutative from noncommutative contracts and identify which contracts fall in each category.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.8","pages":[124,125]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Qard (loan)","Hibah (gift)","Bai' (sale)","Kafalah (guarantee)","Ijarah (lease)"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,3]},{"id":"q-ch5-27","type":"scenario","prompt":"An Islamic bank issues a Letter of Guarantee (Kafalah) for a client. Per the chapter, how may the bank legitimately charge for this?","explanation":"Kafalah is a Tabarru' (noncommutative) contract, so no profit can be charged against it per se; however, banks can charge fees for related services on a Wakalah or Ju'alah basis, and such charges should be amount-based (possibly in slabs), not time-based.","topicId":"t5.8","chapter":5,"section":"5.8.2","topicLabel":"Commutative vs Noncommutative Contracts","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Distinguish commutative from noncommutative contracts and identify which contracts fall in each category.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.8.2","pages":[125]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["By charging a time-based interest rate on the guaranteed amount","It cannot charge anything at all, since Kafalah is gratuitous","By charging an amount-based (e.g. slab-based) fee for the genuine service rendered, on a Wakalah/Ju'alah basis -- not a time-based charge","By charging whatever percentage the market will bear, since Kafalah is a commutative contract"],"correctAnswer":2},{"id":"q-ch5-28","type":"short","prompt":"Define T'aliq, Idafa, and Iqtiran as the three types of contractual stipulation discussed in Section 5.9.","explanation":"These are the three stipulation types the Fiqh literature discusses regarding conditional/contingent contracts.","topicId":"t5.9","chapter":5,"section":"5.9","topicLabel":"Conditional or Contingent Contracts","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain how conditional/contingent stipulations affect the validity of a contract.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.9","pages":[126]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"definition","correctAnswer":"T'aliq = conditions which suspend a contract to a future event. Idafa = an extension that delays the beginning of a contract until a future time. Iqtiran (concomitance) = a condition that varies the terms of the contract."},{"id":"q-ch5-29","type":"scenario","prompt":"A sale contract includes a condition that the buyer must pledge an asset as security for the credit price. Is this a valid condition?","explanation":"The chapter states that a condition 'in aid of' a contract is valid, giving the example of a sale on condition that the buyer pledges something as security for the price.","topicId":"t5.9","chapter":5,"section":"5.9","topicLabel":"Conditional or Contingent Contracts","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain how conditional/contingent stipulations affect the validity of a contract.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.9","pages":[126]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["No, any collateral condition automatically voids a sale","Yes -- a condition requiring a pledge as security for the price is valid because it aids fulfilment of the contract and is explicitly recognized","No, because only Khiyar al-Shart conditions are ever valid","Yes, but only if the pledge is cash"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch5-30","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which school of Fiqh, per the chapter, gives the widest latitude by upholding every stipulation in a contract as long as it does not contradict explicit Qur'anic or Sunnah text?","explanation":"Hanbali jurists emphasize the supremacy of the discretion of contracting parties and allow every condition/stipulation as long as it does not contradict any text from the Qur'an or Sunnah; Hanafi, Shafi'i and Maliki jurists instead sort conditions into valid, irregular and void.","topicId":"t5.9","chapter":5,"section":"5.9","topicLabel":"Conditional or Contingent Contracts","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain how conditional/contingent stipulations affect the validity of a contract.","source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.9","pages":[126]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Hanafi","Shafi'i","Maliki","Hanbali"],"correctAnswer":3}],"flashcards":[{"id":"f5.1","front":"Mal","back":"Anything usable with legal and material value — valuable, possessable, of legitimate use; includes intangible rights like trademarks.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t5.2","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.2","pages":[101,103]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f5.2","front":"Milk ul‘Ain, Milk ud Dayn, Milk ul Manf‘at","back":"Ownership of asset, of debt, of usufruct. Asset ownership includes usufruct, not vice versa; usufruct ownership is time-bound.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t5.2","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.2","pages":[101,103]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f5.3","front":"Mithli vs Qimi goods — which suits Salam and which Istisna‘a?","back":"Mithli (fungible) → Salam; Qimi (non-fungible) → Istisna‘a.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.2","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.2","pages":[101,103]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f5.4","front":"Mithaq vs ‘Ahd/Wa‘dah vs ‘Aqd","back":"Solemn covenant / unilateral promise / contract formed by offer and acceptance.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t5.2.1","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.2.1","pages":[103,105]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f5.5","front":"Three essential elements of a contract","back":"Sighah (offer and acceptance), ‘Aqidain (contracting parties), Ma‘qud ‘alayh (subject matter).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.4","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.4","pages":[106,106]},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["fc-ch5-7"],"backAlt":"Sighah (form: offer and acceptance), 'Aqidain (the contracting parties), and Ma'qud 'alayh (the subject matter)."},{"id":"f5.6","front":"When is an offer cancelled?","back":"Withdrawal; death or loss of capacity; end of the Majlis without conclusion; destruction of the subject matter; lapse of time fixed for acceptance.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.4.1","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.4.1","pages":[106,108]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f5.7","front":"Constructive (Hukmi) possession","back":"The commodity is in the possessor’s risk and control, with rights and liabilities (including risk of destruction) passed to him, without physical delivery.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t5.4.2","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.4.2","pages":[108,110]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f5.8","front":"Four forms of the prohibited ‘two contracts in one’","back":"Compulsory second purchase; one article at two prices unchosen; contingent sale; combining sale and lending.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.5.5","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5.5","pages":[112,113]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f5.9","front":"OIC Fiqh Academy conditions for a binding promise","back":"Unilateral; promisee incurred liabilities/expenses; actual sale by offer and acceptance later; on breach court may enforce purchase or actual damages (no opportunity cost).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.6","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.6","pages":[114,116]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f5.10","front":"Hamish Jiddiyah vs ‘Arbun","back":"HJ: before sale, held in trust, actual loss deducted, rest refunded. ‘Arbun: at execution, part of price, retainable on non-performance (AAOIFI: refund excess over actual loss).","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t5.6.1","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.6.1","pages":[116,117]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f5.11","front":"Sahih, Fasid, Batil","back":"Valid; voidable (valid in Asl, defective in Wasf, curable); void (no effect).","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t5.7","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7","pages":[117,120]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f5.12","front":"Nafiz vs Mawquf; Lazim vs Ghair Lazim","back":"Effective vs suspended pending ratification; binding vs revocable (Wakalah, Kafalah, Shirkah, Wadi‘ah, ‘Ariyah are revocable by nature).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.7","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7","pages":[117,120]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f5.13","front":"Effects of a Fasid sale where goods were delivered with consent","back":"Ownership passes by possession; buyer pays market value; revocable unless disposed of or altered; onward sale valid.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.7.2","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.2","pages":[120,123]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f5.14","front":"Buyu’al Amanat (trust sales)","back":"Tawliyah (at cost), Murabaha (cost plus profit), Wadhi‘ah (at a loss).","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t5.8","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.8","pages":[124,125]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f5.15","front":"How may banks charge for guarantees and L/Cs?","back":"No charge on Kafalah per se; fees for services via Wakalah/Ju‘alah based on expenses — amount-based (slabs possible), not time-based.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t5.8","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.8","pages":[124,125]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f5.16","front":"Effect of a void condition: commutative vs gratuitous contract","back":"Commutative: the contract becomes void. Gratuitous: only the condition is ineffective (e.g. interest clause in a loan — loan stands, principal only).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.8","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.8","pages":[124,125]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f5.17","front":"‘Ain vs Dayn property","back":"‘Ain: a specific, determinate asset. Dayn: indeterminate property owed generically (gold, currency, grain) — any units may be delivered.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t5.2","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.2","pages":[101,103]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f5.18","front":"Bai‘ al ‘Inah vs sale and lease-back","back":"Sale and buy-back (‘Inah) is prohibited by the majority; sale and lease-back is allowed by almost all.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.2","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.2","pages":[101,103]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f5.19","front":"Wording vs intention","back":"In Mu‘amalat, the apparent wording has legal effect; in Ibadat, the intention (Niyyah) is what matters.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t5.3","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.3","pages":[105,106]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f5.20","front":"Sanhuri’s seven components of a contract","back":"Offer–acceptance concurrence; unity of Majlis; plural parties; sanity; deliverable subject matter; defined object; permitted (beneficial) object.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.4","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.4","pages":[106,106]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f5.21","front":"Contracts exempt from unity of session","back":"Agency, gift, and appointment of an executor for a minor’s property.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.4.1","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.4.1","pages":[106,108]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f5.22","front":"Ibn Rushd’s prohibited exchange","back":"Delay on both sides (liability for liability) = debt for debt — prohibited; hence full prepayment in Salam.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.5.5","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5.5","pages":[112,113]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f5.23","front":"Actual loss on breach of promise","back":"Recoverable: actual monetary loss. Not recoverable: opportunity cost / cost of funds.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.6","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.6","pages":[114,116]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f5.24","front":"Mawquf contract — three causes","back":"Defective capacity (minor); lack of authority (Fuduli); right of a third party (e.g. mortgagee).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.7","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7","pages":[117,120]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f5.25","front":"Four types of invalid condition in sale","back":"Against the contract’s purpose; expressly prohibited (sale plus loan); against commercial usage; advantageous to one party at the other’s cost.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.7.2","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.2","pages":[120,123]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f5.26","front":"Ijarah Mosufah bil Zimmah","back":"Lease of a described service/benefit without identifying specific units; loss of one unit does not end the lease.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t5.7.2","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.2","pages":[120,123]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f5.27","front":"Batil vs Fasid: onward sale","back":"Batil: ownership never passes, so the original seller can reclaim even after resale. Fasid with consent and delivery: onward sale is valid.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.7.3","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.3","pages":[123,124]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f5.28","front":"T‘aliq, Idafa, Iqtiran","back":"Suspension on a future event; delay of the start to a future time; concomitant condition varying the terms.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t5.9","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.9","pages":[126,127]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-1","front":"What is Mal in Islamic commercial law?","back":"Anything usable that has legal and material value, is possessable, and has a legitimate use -- includes money, commodities, immovable property and intangible rights like trademarks.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t5.2","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.2","pages":[101]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-2","front":"Distinguish 'Ain from Dayn.","back":"'Ain is determinate/specific property (e.g. a particular house); Dayn is indeterminate property or a liability owed by one person to another, settled by delivering any equivalent units of that kind of property.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t5.2","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.2","pages":[101,102]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-3","front":"Why can't currency notes be the Mabi' (subject of sale) against a homogeneous currency?","back":"Currency notes function as Thaman (price/medium of exchange), not as a commodity -- their exact utility cannot be assessed before they are actually spent.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.2","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.2","pages":[102]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-4","front":"What are the three categories of ownership described in Chapter 5?","back":"Milk ul-'Ain (ownership of the asset), Milk ud-Dayn (ownership of a debt), and Milk ul-Manf'at (ownership of usufruct only).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.2","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.2","pages":[101]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-5","front":"What is the governing principle for Ibadat versus Mu'amalat?","back":"Ibadat (devotional acts) are sanctioned only by express injunction -- what is not commanded cannot be obligatory. Mu'amalat (transactions) are governed by permissibility -- nothing is prohibited unless expressly proscribed by the Qur'an and Sunnah.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t5.3","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.3","pages":[105]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-6","front":"In Mu'amalat, does a party's secret intent (Niyyah) override the words of the contract?","back":"No. In Mu'amalat, the apparent wording/form of the contract is what has legal effect; a party cannot later claim the contract didn't reflect his true intent. (In Ibadat, intent itself is what's judged.)","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.3","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.3","pages":[105]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-8","front":"What does 'unity of Majlis' require for offer and acceptance?","back":"Offer and acceptance should be issued in the same contracting session; based on the Hadith that contracting parties have a right of option until they separate. Some scholars treat the time reasonably taken to communicate acceptance as still part of the same session.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.4.1","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.4.1","pages":[106,107]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-9","front":"Name three events that cause an offer (Ijab) to lapse.","back":"Any of: withdrawal by the maker, death/incapacity of a party, termination of the Majlis without concluding the contract, destruction of the subject matter, or lapse of the time fixed for acceptance.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.4.1","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.4.1","pages":[107]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-10","front":"Why is sale of a debt/receivable generally void, and what makes it valid?","back":"It's void for Gharar -- the creditor doesn't know whether/when the debtor will pay. It becomes valid if structured under the rules of Hawalah (assignment of debt) with recourse to the original debtor.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.4.2","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.4.2","pages":[108]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-11","front":"Define Qabza (Hukmi) -- constructive possession.","back":"A situation where the buyer has not taken physical delivery of the commodity, yet the commodity has come into his risk and control, with all rights and liabilities (including risk of destruction) passed to him.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t5.4.2","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.4.2","pages":[110]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-12","front":"What is Najash?","back":"Bidding up the price of goods without any intention to purchase and take delivery -- a prohibited practice that undermines free consent.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t5.5","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5.1","pages":[110]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-13","front":"Define Gharar and give one example from the text.","back":"Excessive uncertainty about a contract's subject matter or price, potentially causing dispute or unjust enrichment. Example: sale of fish still in the water, or unripe fruit whose quality isn't yet knowable.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t5.5","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5.2","pages":[110,111]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-14","front":"What are the only two Shari'ah-sanctioned exceptions to the rule that the sold item must exist and be possessed?","back":"Salam and Istisna'a -- validated by strict conditions (precise quality, quantity, price, delivery time/place) that remove Gharar.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.5","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5.2","pages":[111]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-15","front":"Distinguish Qimar from Maisir.","back":"Qimar = games of chance/gambling, where possession of property is contingent on an uncertain event. Maisir = getting something too easily, or a profit without working for it.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t5.5","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5.4","pages":[112]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-16","front":"List the four types of prohibited 'two mutually contingent contracts.'","back":"1) Forcing purchase of a second article to get a first; 2) selling one article for two unstipulated prices; 3) contingent sale; 4) combining sale and lending in one contract.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.5.5","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5.5","pages":[112]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-17","front":"Why must Bai' and Ijarah be kept as separate contracts in Ijarah Muntahia-bi-Tamleek?","back":"Because sale transfers ownership and risk to the buyer while Ijarah does not -- only a unilateral, nonbinding-on-the-other-party promise may link the eventual transfer, otherwise the lease-plus-transfer bundling would be void.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.5.5","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5.5","pages":[112,113]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-18","front":"State the principle of Al-Ghunm bil-Ghurm.","back":"Earning profit is legitimized only by risk-sharing and engaging in an economic venture -- one can claim profit only if ready to bear business liability/risk. This is the basis for prohibiting a guaranteed return on lent money.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t5.5.7","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5.7","pages":[113]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-19","front":"What is Ibahatul Asliyah?","back":"General permissibility -- all economic activities not prohibited by the Qur'an and Sunnah are valid/permissible; this underlies the validity of deferred-payment and forward transactions.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t5.5","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5.8","pages":[114]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-20","front":"When does a unilateral commercial promise (Wa'dah) become legally binding, per contemporary consensus?","back":"When the promisee has relied on it by incurring expenses or taking on liability -- the OIC Fiqh Academy also requires it be unilateral, that the actual sale still occur via fresh offer/acceptance, and default remedies be limited to compelled performance or actual (not opportunity-cost) damages.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.6","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.6","pages":[114,115]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-21","front":"Distinguish Hamish Jiddiyah from 'Arbun.","back":"Hamish Jiddiyah is earnest money taken BEFORE execution of the sale agreement (held in trust, adjusted into price, actual-loss recovery on default). 'Arbun is a downpayment taken AFTER execution of the sale agreement, as part of the price.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t5.6.1","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.6.1","pages":[116]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-22","front":"Which school of Fiqh treats Bai' al-'Arbun as a valid independent sale contract?","back":"Only the Hanbali school; other schools do not consider it valid as a separate sale contract, though a stipulated forfeitable advance within an actual sale can be acceptable to the majority.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t5.6.1","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.6.1","pages":[116]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-23","front":"What's the difference between Nafiz and Mawquf contracts?","back":"Nafiz = immediately effective on execution. Mawquf = effectiveness suspended until a future happening, e.g. ratification (minor's contract, Fuduli's unauthorized act, or a third party's/mortgagee's right).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.7","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.1","pages":[118]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-24","front":"What's the difference between Lazim and Ghair Lazim contracts?","back":"Lazim = binding; neither party can unilaterally revoke without the other's consent. Ghair Lazim = nonbinding; either party may revoke unilaterally (e.g. Wakalah, Kafalah, Shirkah, Wadi'ah, 'Ariyah by nature, or any contract with a stipulated Khiyar al-Shart).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.7","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.1","pages":[119]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-25","front":"Define a Fasid (voidable) contract.","back":"A contract that is legal in its 'Asl (has all essential elements) but illegal in its Wasf (external/nonessential attributes); it is not automatically void, but can be regularized by removing the cause of irregularity.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t5.7.2","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.2","pages":[120]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-26","front":"In a Fasid sale where the buyer takes possession with the seller's consent, at what value does ownership pass?","back":"At market value (or mutually agreed value), not necessarily the price originally fixed in the agreement -- unlike a valid contract, where ownership transfers at the agreed price by mere offer and acceptance.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t5.7.2","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.2","pages":[123]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-27","front":"Define a Batil (void) contract and its legal effect.","back":"A contract that fails major conditions on offer/acceptance, subject matter, consideration, or possession, or has illegal external attributes. It produces no legal effect at all -- ownership never transfers, and delivered goods must be returned even from a third-party buyer.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t5.7.3","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.3","pages":[123,124]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-28","front":"Give an example of a Batil (void) sale from the text.","back":"Sale of milk still in a cow's udder (absolute uncertainty/speculation), or sale of a dirham for two dirhams.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.7.3","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.3","pages":[123]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-29","front":"Distinguish Uqood-e-Mu'awadha from Uqood Ghair Mu'awadha.","back":"Mu'awadha (commutative) contracts allow legitimate compensation, e.g. Bai', Ijarah, Wakalah. Ghair Mu'awadha/Tabarru' (noncommutative/gratuitous) contracts do not allow compensation, e.g. Qard, Hibah, Kafalah, Hawalah.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t5.8","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.8","pages":[124]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-30","front":"How can Islamic banks legitimately earn fees on Kafalah, Qard, or Hawalah, given they can't charge profit on these Tabarru' contracts directly?","back":"By charging fees for genuinely separate services on a Wakalah or Ju'alah basis (e.g. for issuing L/Cs or guarantees) -- these fees should be amount-based (e.g. slabs), not time-based.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t5.8","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.8.2","pages":[125]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-31","front":"What happens to a commutative contract versus a Tabarru' contract when a void condition is inserted?","back":"A commutative (compensatory) contract becomes void; a noncompensatory (Tabarru') contract does not -- only the void condition itself becomes ineffective, and the underlying contract (e.g. a loan) remains valid and enforceable.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.8","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.8.3","pages":[125]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-32","front":"Distinguish T'aliq, Idafa, and Iqtiran.","back":"T'aliq = conditions suspending a contract to a future event. Idafa = an extension delaying the start of a contract until a future time. Iqtiran (concomitance) = a condition that varies the terms of the contract.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t5.9","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.9","pages":[126]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch5-33","front":"Can a sale contract be made effective from a future date?","back":"No -- per the majority of jurists, a sale must be immediately effective (delay introduces Gharar). Ijarah, Istisna'a, Kafalah and Hawalah, however, can validly be effective from a future date. (Ibn Taymiyah and Ibn al-Qayyim dissent for sale too.)","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t5.7","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.1","pages":[118,119]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}],"exam":[{"id":"e5.1","kind":"long","prompt":"Discuss the elements of a valid contract in Islamic law, with particular reference to the conditions of the subject matter.","expectedStructure":["Three essential elements (and Sanhuri’s seven)","Offer and acceptance rules; unity of session; Khiyar al-Shart","Capacity of parties","Subject matter conditions: existence, lawfulness, ownership, possession, deliverability, determination, price","Constructive possession","Exceptions: Salam, Istisna‘a; Hawalah for debts"],"keyConcepts":["Sighah","Qabza","Gharar","Mabi‘/Thaman"],"essentialPoints":["No short-selling","Mortgaged asset cannot be sold","Price must be fixed"],"commonMistakes":["Omitting possession","Forgetting Salam exception"],"topicId":"t5.4.2","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.4.2","pages":[108,110]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e5.2","kind":"difference","prompt":"Differentiate between Sahih, Fasid and Batil contracts with examples and legal effects.","expectedStructure":["Definitions (Asl/Wasf)","Causes","Examples","Effects on ownership, price, onward sale","Hanafi vs other schools"],"keyConcepts":["Asl","Wasf","market value"],"essentialPoints":["Fasid curable","Batil no effect"],"commonMistakes":["Treating Fasid and Batil as identical"],"topicId":"t5.7.2","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.7.2","pages":[120,123]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e5.3","kind":"short","prompt":"Is a promise binding in Islamic commercial law? Explain with the OIC Fiqh Academy conditions and an example.","expectedStructure":["‘Ahd/Wa‘dah defined","Juristic views","OIC conditions","Actual loss example","Bilateral promise"],"keyConcepts":["Wa‘dah","actual loss","Muwa‘adah"],"essentialPoints":["Opportunity cost excluded"],"commonMistakes":["Treating the promise as the sale"],"topicId":"t5.6","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.6","pages":[114,116]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e5.4","kind":"conceptual","prompt":"Why does Shari’ah prohibit ‘two contracts in one’, and how do Islamic banks combine contracts lawfully?","expectedStructure":["Four prohibited forms","Rationale: undefined rights/liabilities (Gharar)","Permissible combinations with conditions","Debt-for-debt prohibition"],"keyConcepts":["IMBT","Diminishing Musharakah","Wakalah"],"essentialPoints":["Separate contracts","Unilateral promise"],"commonMistakes":["Saying all combinations are prohibited"],"topicId":"t5.5.5","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.5.5","pages":[112,113]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e5.5","kind":"viva","prompt":"What is the difference between commutative and gratuitous contracts? Give examples.","expectedStructure":["Definition","Lists","Fees for guarantees"],"keyConcepts":["Mu‘awadha","Tabarru‘"],"essentialPoints":["Amount-based not time-based fees"],"commonMistakes":["Listing Wakalah as purely gratuitous"],"topicId":"t5.8","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.8","pages":[124,125]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e5.6","kind":"scenario","prompt":"A client promises to buy machinery from the bank at cost plus 10%, pays token money, and then withdraws after the bank has bought the machine. Advise the bank on its rights.","expectedStructure":["Nature of the promise (Wa‘dah)","Binding promise: OIC conditions","Hamish Jiddiyah vs ‘Arbun","Measuring actual loss (no cost of funds)","Refund of the excess","Conclusion"],"keyConcepts":["Wa‘dah","Hamish Jiddiyah","‘Arbun","actual loss","OIC Fiqh Academy"],"essentialPoints":["Promise was unilateral and caused the bank expense","Bank sells the machine and claims the shortfall","Token money held as trust"],"commonMistakes":["Keeping the full token money","Charging cost of funds","Treating the promise as a sale"],"topicId":"t5.6.1","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.6.1","pages":[116,117]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"et-21","kind":"answer-plan","prompt":"List the essential elements of a valid contract (Aqd) and explain the broad rules governing the validity of Mu'amalat (transactions) in Islamic law.","expectedStructure":["State the three essential elements: form (offer and acceptance/Sighah), contracting parties (Aqidain), subject matter (Ma'qud 'alayh)","Note the conditions on offer and acceptance (clear, unconditional, same session/Majlis)","Note the conditions on subject matter (existing, valuable, deliverable, specified, owned)","Cover the broad Mu'amalat validity rules: free mutual consent, absence of Gharar, avoidance of Riba, avoidance of Qimar/Maisir, no two mutually contingent contracts, conformity with Maqasid al-Shari'ah, profit with liability, and permissibility as a general rule"],"keyConcepts":["Aqd","Ijab and Qabul","Gharar","Mu'amalat","Majlis"],"essentialPoints":["A valid contract requires three essential elements: the form (offer/Ijab and acceptance/Qabul, i.e. Sighah), the contracting parties (Aqidain) who must be mature and sane, and the subject matter (Ma'qud 'alayh); Sanhuri's expanded list also requires unity of session, plurality of parties, sanity, deliverability of the subject matter, a defined object, and its beneficial/lawful nature.","The subject matter must exist (or be existable, as in Salam), have value, be usable and lawful, capable of ownership and delivery, and specifically identified; the price must be certain and fixed once and for all at the time of contract.","The broad rules for validity of Mu'amalat are: free mutual consent (free of oppression, fraud, Najash, Ghaban-e-Fahish); prohibition of Gharar (excessive uncertainty); avoiding Riba; avoiding Qimar and Maisir; prohibition of combining two mutually contingent/inconsistent contracts in one bargain; conformity with the Maqasid of Shari'ah; entitlement to profit only with liability for loss; and permissibility as the general default rule (whatever is not expressly prohibited is permitted).","Some contract combinations remain permissible under conditions (e.g. Shirkah combined with Ijarah, or Wakalah/Kafalah combined with a sale or lease), as long as each contract's own rights and liabilities are respected and the contracts are not made interdependent."],"commonMistakes":["Listing only the three structural elements and forgetting the separate, cross-cutting Mu'amalat validity rules (Gharar, Riba, Maisir, contingent contracts, Maqasid conformity, profit-with-liability, permissibility).","Assuming any combination of contracts is prohibited outright — the book allows specific combinations (e.g. Diminishing Musharakah's Shirkah plus Ijarah) as long as their respective conditions are met."],"topicId":"t5.4","chapter":5,"source":{"chapter":5,"section":"5.4","pages":[106,110,111,112]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}]});
